\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar hits new low after weak jobs data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masaru Sato \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 1998\par 18:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, July 10 (Reuters) - Canada's limping dollar received another blow on Friday, when nervous selling sparked by surprisingly weak employment data sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to record lows. \par \par \uc2  Before trimming some losses, the Canadian dollar fell to a record trading low of C$1.4795 (US$0.6759) on Friday. \par \par \uc2  A week ago, it stood at C$1.4690 to the U.S. dollar (US$0.6807). \par \par \uc2  Traders, who had expected some growth in job creation, sold Canadian dollars on news that the country's employment fell for the second consecutive month in June. \par \par \uc2  Total employment fell by 35,900 to 14.24 million with most declines recorded in the agricultural sector, after a drop of 7,300 in May. The jobless rate was unchanged at 8.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Canadians may see more weakness on the jobs front in July as the impact of the General Motors plant shutdowns in North America was not fully reflected in the latest data. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada, the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has so far stood by and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide because its retreat has been fairly orderly. \par \par \uc2  Economists and market participants expect the bank to hold off raising its key lending rate for a while. Such a move would would be expected to lure investors into Canadian assets, boosting dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Mario Angastiniotis, senior economist at Standard & Poor's MMS, said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell U.S. dollars around the key psychological level of C$1.4800 (US$0.6757). \par \par \uc2  "As long as there's no momentum behind to push it (the U.S. dollar) to higher spot levels (against the Canadian dollar), I think the bank will basically let it ride without doing anything on the interest rate front," he told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  The only support for the Canadian dollar appears to be coming from occasional slips in the U.S. dollar's value against key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the German mark and Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell against the mark on Friday as the market turned more optimistic after Thursday's bearish views of Russia's economic problems. This pulled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to 140 yen, albeit for a short period, from above 141 yen. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets remained wary because of uncertainty over the prospect of Japan's ruling party under Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto surviving the Upper House election on Sunday and delivering on promised fiscal stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar is vulnerable to safe-haven capital flows to the United States. The U.S. dollar has in the past firmed against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when concerns grew over the world's troubled regions, such as Asia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar story has been a global story until today. The weakness in the exchange rate was clearly reflecting the weakness in the commodities prices, which are relevant to Canada," said Andrew Spence, senior economist at Deutsche Bank Securities. "However, we're beginning to see signs of slowdown domestically, and the question is if that slowdown is temporary or something more permanent." \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts foresee further medium-term weakness in the Canadian dollar, which has been sliding since mid-March when it peaked at C$1.4073 (US$0.7106) in the current cycle. \par \par \uc2  Canada's bearish jobs report came on the heels of Thursday's news of a 4.6 percent drop in June housing starts, making the third consecutive month of declines. Construction industry strikes in the key province of Ontario were blamed. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7a00zzv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
