\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds open weaker as U.S. dlr, bonds fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,087 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 18 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds opened weaker on Friday, moving in a tight range ahead of the weekend, as some funds flowed back into equities and a weaker U.S. dollar hurt the sentiment of North American bonds. \par \par \uc2  A fall in the U.S. dollar to 134 yen on Friday after a rebound to 138 yen on Thursday was depressing U.S. treasuries, which opened lower on profit-taking overseas. \par \par \uc2  U.S. bonds have gained sharply in recent weeks from the notion that economic crises in Asia and Russia would continue to drive global capital seeking a safe haven into U.S. dollar assets. \par \par \uc2  "We are mostly following the U.S. market today. The price action is... there's not whole a lot going on," said Jeoff Hall, managing Canadian market analyst at Technical Data in Boston. "The trend lower comes in lower volume." \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond fell C$0.37 to C$135.37 to yield 5.534 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. 30-year counterpart fell 12/32 to yield 5.74 percent. The U.S.-Canada spread was 21 basis points after 20 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  Bonds are down as some expect a further fall in the U.S. dollar against the yen as deputies to the Group of Seven finance ministers gather in Tokyo to discuss a broad range of economic issues in Japan and Asia. \par \par \uc2  Positive statements by Summers on Tokyo's bid to jump-start its economy and implement structural reform could trigger more buying of yen versus the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Some nervousness stemmed from talk that two ailing Japanese banks, Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan and Nippon Credit Bank, would be delisted, Hall said. The two banks denied a report of a possible merger between the two overnight. \par \par \uc2  Canada's five-year bond was falling ahead of U.S. two-and five-year note auctions next week. \par \par \uc2  When the U.S. dollar was forging ahead against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  until Washington and Tokyo staged a rare joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, it wasn't just \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problem. \par \par \uc2  But now the Canadian dollar doesn't rally when the yen rallies, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are asking themselves what will happen to the Canadian dollar when the yen goes back down to 145 yen to the U.S. dollar? \par \par \uc2  And this weakness sometimes spills into Canadian bonds. \par \par \uc2  "Right now the Bank of Canada has really got its back up against the wall right now," Hall said. "There was a lot of consideration given to today being the day when the Bank of Canada would hike rates, and didn't happen, so the Canadian dollar was an easy target for sellers." \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's a long-term phenomenon, but as we get into next week, Monday and Tuesday, I think that the rate watch vigil gets all in earnest." he predicted. \par \par \uc2  Hall said he did not agree with a rate increase at this stage to boost the Canadian dollar, but added that could be "a necessary evil." \par \par \uc2  "To be consistent with their prior action and words, then certainly it looks like a rate hike would justified," he said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar has been under pressure from weak prices of commodities, a pillar of the nation's exports, and as capital flows into safer and more attractive U.S. assets, which provide higher returns than Canadian bonds. \par \par \uc2  Canada's May consumer price index (CPI) released on Friday were above the consensus forecast. \par \par \uc2  Overall CPI rose 1.1 percent year on year in May after a 0.8 percent rise in April, and the core rate (ex-food and energy) rose 1.4 percent from a year earlier after a 1.2 percent rise in April. \par \par \uc2  Economists on average had predicted a rise of 0.7 percent in all items year on year, and an increase of 1.2 percent in the core rate. \par \par \uc2  "I think what we are seeing is a transitory price increase, certain areas stemming from the weaker Canadian dollar, one area specifically the travel accommodation cost. That is due to increased tourism from the U.S.," said Sal Guatieri, senior economist at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's gain versus the Canadian dollar in recent months gives Americans higher purchasing power. \par \par \uc2  "No increase in underlying price pressures. Goods inflation on a year-on-year basis is still very subdued. Even services inflation was quite tame," he said. "That of course is reflecting an ongoing slack in the economy and falling commodity prices." \par \par \uc2  Mark Mullins, chief economist at Midland Walwyn Capital Inc said the business travel cost, natural gas and food prices contributed to the higher inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  "I think we going to find these more a one-off factor than a new trend. I think we're still going to see the numbers basically below one percent annualized for the next few months," he told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  On whether higher-than-expected CPI would justify an interest rate hike by the Bank of Canada for supporting the Canadian dollar, he said: \par \par \uc2  "We are dealing with the situation here in Canada there is a very high degree of uncertainty. And it's more international and domestic. The (\b \highlight22\uc2 central) bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently reduced its forecast for economic growth. So I don't think they are in any great rush to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  The money market was steady to a bit weaker as the Canadian dollar remained under selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  The market was also watching for any G7 comments on Japan and Asia. \par \par \uc2  Canada's inflation rate, as shown in the CPI, is still in a good range and there's no extreme worry that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be forced to raise interest rates, one trader said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the market really expects the bank to (raise rates now), just keeping a wary eye on it," he said. "We're just sitting here on the edge of what's happening. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill traded with a yield of 4.85 percent, compared with 4.84 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was moving between C$1.4732 (US$0.6788) and C$.4685 (US$0.6810) in nervous trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada holds its twice-monthly T-bill auction next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02mo1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
