\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar opens flat in nervous trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4630/1.4720     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4693 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4550 RESISTANCE 1.4750 HIGH 1.4725 LOW 1.4678 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4702 HIGH 1.4725\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4682\par PREV CLOSE  1.4707 LOW  1.4678\tab HIGH 1.4723 LOW 1.4692\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JUNE 19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada dollar opened flat around C$1.4702 (US$0.6802), in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par volatile and nervous trading on anticipation by some of a Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Canada rate rise. *Yen, mark, others rose vs USD, but Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par left out of game. *Canada May CPI rise higher than forecast,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par but data seen as one-off factor.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 19 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened flat around C$1.4702 (US$0.6802) on Friday, but trading was erratic and nervous ahead of the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT window for the Bank of Canada money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen's rebound against the U.S. dollar to 134 yen from 138 yen on Thursday has not helped the Canadian unit much this morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it's because of the nine o'clock shuffle. People are feeling a bit under pressure, a weakness in Canada with the yen rally, Aussie rally, everything is rallying against the (U.S.) dollar except for Canada," one trader said. "People are viewing this as a possibility that there might be a move by the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central) bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expects the Canadian dollar to weaken again towards C$1.4750 (US$0.6780) in the absence of support from higher interest rates and higher returns on investment in Canadian assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has opted to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  periodically in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem any drastic fall by the Canadian dollar, but has not pushed up key lending rates because the slide has been orderly rather than panicky.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's inflation rate came in higher than expected in May, but economists see this as a one-off event, not indicating any risk to price stability in future.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit fell to 93.31 yen from 93.65 yen at the previous close here as the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gained against the U.S. dollar, and remained flat against the German \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , quoted at 1.2185 marks after 1.2188 marks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The market is cautiously awaiting more news from Tokyo,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par where Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers and other\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. officials are meeting top Japanese policymakers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     One U.S. official said overnight the next three weeks up to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's Upper House election would be crucial as there is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par always a danger of a political vacuum amid election campaigns.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Japan faces the daunting task of regaining investor\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par confidence in the reform of its corrupt and inefficient banking\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par system and in the use of macroeconomic policy to turn its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ailing economy around as quickly as possible.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Canada's May consumer price index (CPI) figures, released\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par earlier on Friday, were above consensus forecasts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Overall CPI rose 1.1 percent year on year in May after a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0.8 percent rise in April, and the core rate (ex-food and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par energy) rose 1.4 percent from a year earlier after a 1.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent rise in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists on average had predicted a rise of 0.7 percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in all items year on year, and an increase of 1.2 percent in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the core rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think what we are seeing is a transitory price increase,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par certain areas stemming from the weaker Canadian dollar, one\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par area specifically the travel accommodation cost. That is due to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par increased tourism from the U.S.," said Sal Guatieri, senior\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economist at Bank of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The U.S. dollar's gain against the Canadian dollar in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recent months gives Americans more purchasing power.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "No increase in underlying price pressures. Goods inflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on a year-on-year basis is still very subdued. Even services\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation was quite tame," he said. "That of course is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par reflecting an ongoing slack in the economy and falling\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par commodity prices."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mark Mullins, chief economist at Midland Walwyn Capital Inc\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said the business travel cost, natural gas and food prices\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par contributed to the higher inflation rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think we going to find these more a one-off factor than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a new trend. I think we're still going to see the numbers\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par basically below one percent annualized for the next few\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par months," he told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On whether higher-than-expected CPI would justify an\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par interest rate hike by the Bank of Canada for supporting the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian dollar, he said:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are dealing with the situation here in Canada there is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a very high degree of uncertainty. And it's more international\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and domestic. The (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central) bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has recently reduced its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par forecast for economic growth. So I don't think they are in any\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par great rush to raise rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to have stepped\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par into the market to buy Canada from C$1.4685 (US$0.6810) to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.4705 (US$0.6800), and met constant U.S. dollar buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A smaller-than-forecast surplus in Canada's international\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trade weighed on the local dollar on Thursday as traders took\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par it as a sign of slower economic growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April trade surplus fell to C$1.20 billion from a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par revised C$1.81 billion in March, below the average forecast by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economists at C$1.69 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02miy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
