\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends flat after choppy, seesaw trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 1998\par 22:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4630/1.4720     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4693 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4550 RESISTANCE 1.4750 HIGH 1.4725 LOW 1.4678 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4706 HIGH 1.4732\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4682\par PREV CLOSE  1.4704 LOW  1.4670\tab HIGH 1.4723 LOW 1.4692\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JUNE 19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH ACTIVITY: CHOPPY\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended flat around C$1.4707 (US$0.6799) in nervous trade as the U.S. dollar jumped in afternoon trade. *USD was up vs yen on Rubin's comment that there will be no policy actions from G7 deputy ministers meeting in Tokyo this weekend. *Clinton repeated Tokyo's pledge to take quick action on reform.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Earlier, yen, mark and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose vs USD, but Canada was left out of the game.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's higher-than-forecast CPI rise is seen as one-off factor, but some players jittery over possible Bank of Canada rate hike to boost CAD soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's monetary conditions index down to -5.63 from -5.61 a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 19 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended flat around C$1.4707 (US$0.6799) in nervous choppy trade on Friday after the U.S. dollar jumped by two yen on comments by a top U.S. policymaker.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Mostly it's been quite choppy, not too much direction, going back and forth," one trader said. "As we go into next week, some strategic guys are probably still long U.S. dollars, and inter-bank guys are probably squared."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market players would become more nervous about a possible Bank of Canada rate hike if inflation data continued to rise next month, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's May inflation rate, released on Friday, came in higher than expected but economists see it as a one-off rise, offering no risk to price stability.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the dollar/yen front, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, seeking to reduce the market's high expectations for Group of Seven (G7) solutions to Japan's economic problems, said there will be no policy actions from G7 deputy ministers meeting in Tokyo this weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We have said from the very beginning there will not be policy actions," Rubin told Reuters in an interview on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, after rounds of crisis meetings with Japanese policymakers in Tokyo this week, also meet his G7 colleagues to discuss challenges facing Japanese and other Asian economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan faces the daunting task of regaining investor confidence by reforming its corrupt and inefficient banking system and changing macro-economic policy to stimulate its economy before Asia plunges further into an economic downturn. "These are complex problems... They developed over a long period of time and they may well take time to go through and develop and implement the programmes," Rubin said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Earlier in the day the yen's rebound against the U.S. dollar to 134 yen from 138 yen on Thursday did not help the Canadian unit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jitters ran through the market as players thought Canada was left out of the game, and some anticipated some kind of action by the Bank of Canada to boost the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market was particularly jumpy before the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT window for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's daily money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People are feeling a bit under pressure, with weakness in Canada even with the yen rally, the Aussie rally, everything is rallying against the (U.S.) dollar except for Canada," another trader said. "People are viewing this as a possibility, that there might be a move by the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central) bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expected the Canadian dollar to weaken again towards C$1.4750 (US$0.6780) if there is no support from a higher interest rate, which would offer a higher return on investment in Canadian assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has opted to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  periodically to stem the fall in the Canadian dollar, but has not pushed up the key lending rate as the slide has been orderly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit fell to 92.55 yen from 93.65 yen at the previous close here as the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  generally gained versus the U.S. dollar, and slipped against the German \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , quoted at 1.2154 marks after 1.2188 marks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As the U.S. dollar was forging ahead against all major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Washington and Tokyo staged a rare joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian dollar was not alone in falling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But on Friday the Canadian dollar did not rally when the yen rallied, and\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers are asking themselves what will happen to Canada when the yen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par goes back down to 145 yen to the U.S. dollar?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even commodity price-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were firming versus the greenback\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Australian dollar, though shedding earlier gains, rose to US$0.6172\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from US$0.6078 on Thursday, and the New Zealand dollar was up to US$0.5230 from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$0.5153.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists see Canada's inflation as still being benign.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's overall consumer price index (CPI) for May rose 1.1 percent year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on year after a 0.8 percent rise in April, and the core rate (ex-food and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par energy) rose 1.4 percent from a year earlier after a 1.2 percent rise in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists on average had predicted a rise of 0.7 percent in all items year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on year, and an increase of 1.2 percent in the core rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think what we are seeing is a transitory price increase, certain areas\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stemming from the weaker Canadian dollar, one area specifically, the travel\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par accommodation cost. That is due to increased tourism from the U.S.," said Sal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Guatieri, senior economist at Bank of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar's gain versus the Canadian dollar in recent months gives\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Americans higher purchasing power in Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "(There's) no increase in underlying price pressures. Goods inflation on a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year-on-year basis is still very subdued. Even services inflation was quite\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par tame," he said. "That of course is reflecting ongoing slack in the economy and falling commodity prices."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mark Mullins, chief economist at Midland Walwyn Capital Inc., said the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par business travel cost, natural gas and food prices contributed to the higher inflation rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think we're going to find this more a one-off factor than a new trend. I\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par think we're still going to see the numbers basically below one percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par annualized for the next few months," he told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On whether higher-than-expected CPI would justify an interest rate hike by the Bank of Canada to support the Canadian dollar, he said: "We are dealing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with the situation here in Canada where there is a very high degree of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par uncertainty. And it's more international than domestic. The (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central) bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recently reduced its forecast for economic growth. So I don't think they are in any great rush to raise rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada said on Friday its monetary conditions index (MCI) fell to -5.63 from -5.61 a week earlier. The MCI has been on the downtrend for the last two months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to have stepped into the market to buy Canada from C$1.4685 (US$0.6810) to C$1.4705 (US$0.6800), but it met constant U.S. dollar buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, a smaller-than-forecast surplus in Canada's international\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trade weighed on the Canadian dollar as traders took it as a sign of slower economic growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April trade surplus had fallen to C$1.20 billion from a revised C$1.81 billion in March, below the average forecast by economists at C$1.69 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6j02brq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
