\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds end mixed, long end recovers. \b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 23:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 18 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds ended mixed on Thursday, with the long end of the yield curve recovering from a drop caused by a smaller-than-expected surplus in Canada's international trade for April. \par \par \uc2  While a rebound in the U.S. dollar against yen was prompting buying of U.S. treasuries, a weakening Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overshadowed bonds here in the morning, and was still hurting the short end by the close. \par \par \uc2  "Canada's long end was benefiting from the U.S. and the rest of the market is under pressure because of the (Canadian) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rebounded to a day's high of 138.22 yen on Thursday after plunging to 136 yen from 143 yen on Wednesday in the wake of a rare joint U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar finished North American trade weaker at C$1.4707 (US$0.6799) on Thursday, shedding Wednesday's gains, which were ushered in by the joint action by the world's two biggest economies. \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reacted the least favourably to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  package yesterday and this morning's trade number was extremely poor," said Jim Webber, director of fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. "So those two things together weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,, and in turn weighs on the (bond) market." \par \par \uc2  Canada's April trade surplus fell to C$1.20 billion from a revised C$1.81 billion in March, below an average forecast by economists of C$1.69 billion. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. April trade deficit rose 9.5 percent to $14.46 billion, the highest level since the current series began in January 1992. The U.S.-Japan trade gap fell 6.0 percent to $5.41 billion, while the U.S. deficit with Canada rose 44.2 percent to $1.25 billion. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond rose C$0.30 to C$135.79 to yield 5.511 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. 30-year counterpart rose 13/32 to yield 5.71 percent. The U.S.-Canada spread was 20 basis points after 21 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar remained under pressure from weak prices for crude oil and other commodities as the nation depends on exports of natural resources for steady economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Webber said an interest rate increase by the Bank of Canada would boost the Canadian dollar, but that he did not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates soon. U.S. treasuries were watching movements in the U.S. dollar and the equities market. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average, after rallying on Wednesday, closed down 16.45 points at 8813 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Canada will release its May consumer price index. Economists on average forecast a rise of 0.7 percent in all items year on year after a rise of 0.8 percent in April, and an increase of 1.2 percent in the core rate (ex-food and energy) after a rise of 1.2 percent in April. \par \par \uc2  The government of Canada said on Thursday it will hold four bond auctions, including one real return issue, in the third quarter of 1998. The first auction, of 10-year bonds, will be on August 12, with details to be released on August 6. \par \par \uc2  In regional news, the province of Ontario said there was no T-bill auction on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The money market was weaker, reacting to a fall in the Canadian dollar on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "With the fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened off, we have weakened off as well. We've seen profit-taking in some of the WI bills and in the long end," one money market trader said. \par \par \uc2  "Even when we improved yesterday, our clients were sitting on the sidelines. They want to see where the dollar's going," the trader said, adding that money market players are comfortable with the U.S. dollar below C$1.4640 (US$0.6831). \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar ended weaker at C$1.4707 (US$0.6799) on Thursday, despite talk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reflecting the U.S. dollar's rebound against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Between now and Tuesday, we're expecting to be a little cheaper here," the trader said. The Bank of Canada holds a T-bill auction next Tuesday, which comes in every two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill traded with a yield of 4.84 percent, compared with 4.77 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02lfs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
