\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar ends lower despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 22:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4630/1.4720     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4621 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4550 RESISTANCE 1.4750 HIGH 1.4651 LOW 1.4597 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4648 HIGH 1.4708\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4636\par PREV CLOSE  1.4609 LOW  1.4639\tab HIGH 1.4649 LOW 1.4612\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, JUNE 18\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended weaker around C$1.4702 (US$0.6802), despite talk of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , reflecting the U.S. dollar's rebound above 138\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par yen. *Canada's smaller-than-forecast April trade surplus also weighed on CAD.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Market awaits news from Tokyo, where Treasury's Summers is discussing Japan's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banking reform and economic recovery plans. *Canada's April trade surplus was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par down to C$1.20 billion vs economists' forecasts of C$1.69 billion. *The U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April trade deficit rose 9.5 pct to $14.46 billion, its highest since the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par current series began in Jan 1992. *U.S.-Japan trade gap down 6.0 pct to $5.41\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par billion, U.S.-Canada gap up 44.2 pct to $1.25 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 18 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended weaker around C$1.4702 (US$0.6802) on Thursday, despite talk of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , reflecting the U.S. dollar's rebound against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to have stepped into the market to buy Canadian dollars from C$1.4685 (US$0.6810) to C$1.4705 (US$0.6800), but it met constant U.S. dollar buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Yesterday there were (U.S.) dollar buyers, today there are dollar buyers and cross Canada selling. The market is just steadily bid and it still needs dollars," said one trader. "It's been a pretty tough trend because you just haven't had any time to buy dollars on dips."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market has been disappointed that the bank hasn't had anything (to say) to express concern over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said. "Probably it could have come in with a strong statement."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar rebounded to a day's high of 138.22 yen on Thursday after plunging to 136 yen from 143 yen on Wednesday in the wake of a rare U.S.-Japan joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As long as the yen continues to move back towards 140 (yen to the U.S. dollar), Canada will move towards C$1.4800," said Reid Farrill, executive director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities. "The weakness in the yen will be translated in the weakness in the Canadian dollar."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Figures showing a smaller-than-forecast surplus in Canada's international trade for April, released on Thursday, also weighed on the local dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April trade surplus fell to C$1.20 billion from a revised C$1.81 billion in March, below an average forecast by economists of C$1.69 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A smaller surplus could suggest slower economic growth, but it is a little too early to revise down the estimate for Canada's gross domestic product (GDP) in the second quarter based on this figure, said Randall Powley, senior economist at Scotia Capital Markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's only one month of a quarter. Also I'd like to see some revisions, as we've seen in the recent past, these revisions could be quite large and could change the direction of the impact on the GDP," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. April trade deficit rose 9.5 percent to $14.46 billion, the highest level since the current series began in January 1992. The U.S.-Japan trade gap fell 6.0 percent to $5.41 billion, while the U.S. deficit with Canada rose 44.2 percent to $1.25 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit, after plunging on Wednesday, rose to 93.65 yen from 93.23 yen at the previous close here, but fell to 1.2188 marks from 1.2217 marks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market is awaiting news from Tokyo, where Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is discussing Japan's banking sector reform and plans to haul the flagging Japanese economy out of recession, a key to stabilising Asian economies and financial markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The market wants to see how Summers and his team emerge from a series of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par meetings in Japan early next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "If they come back smiling and with big programmes that everybody believes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par will save Japan, then I think that the (U.S.) dollar will continue to go down\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and the yen will start to rise," Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Frequency Economics told Reuters Television. "But I don't think Japan can\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par deliver the goods and therefore I expect it (the U.S. dollar) to come back\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par firming... I think the mid-140s is back in everybody's sights again."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To regain investor confidence, Japan needs to implement long-term\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par structural reform and deregulation, which will not take place overnight.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's regaining of some ground versus the U.S. dollar is giving the Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Canada some breathing space, but the Canadian dollar remains vulnerable to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par global market uncertainties and weak commodities prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, Canada will release its May consumer price index. Economists on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par average forecast a rise of 0.7 percent in all items year on year after a rise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of 0.8 percent in April, and an increase of 1.2 percent in the core rate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (ex-food and energy) after a rise of 1.2 percent in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02l86\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
