\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 17:30\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market repeatedly Monday morning to\par sell U.S. dollars at C$1.4735 and C$1.4740 in an effort to\par smooth out trading action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The U.S. dollar levelled off briefly after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and was hovering at C$1.4733 at 11:24 a.m. EDT\par (1524 GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after intraday moves in either direction of\par 70 ticks or more in order to contain volatility. The U.S.\par dollar closed at C$1.4663 on Friday.\par    In cases of sustained pressure against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the\par Bank will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly at each 5-tick level.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market sources reported seeing a pattern of\par repeated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday morning\par after the first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.4735 around 10:30 a.m.\par EDT (1430 GMT).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Canadian dollar has been under severe downward\par pressure in recent sessions, setting an all-time lows on\par Thursday and then again this morning.\par    Analysts blame the recent spate of weakness in the\par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the low commodity prices and global\par uncertainties stemming from the renewed economic and\par financial turmoil in Asia.\par    While the Canadian dollar rebounded slightly on Friday\par in the wake of profit taking moves, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  resumed its\par downward slide Monday morning. The intense pressure on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to last until the pressures originating\par in Asia relent, or the Bank of Canada raises interest rates\par to defend the embattled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Some market watchers say sustained negative yield\par spreads between Canada and the U.S. are also a key reason\par for the Canadian dollar's weakness.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-15-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1130EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du6f01gc6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
