\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends weaker vs U.S., hits new lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 968 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 22:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4660/1.4750     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4686 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4640 RESISTANCE 1.4800 HIGH 1.4703 LOW 1.4674 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4699 HIGH 1.4740\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4684\par PREV CLOSE  1.4665 LOW  1.4689\tab HIGH 1.4698 LOW 1.4662\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MONDAY, JUNE 15\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended weaker around C$1.4735 (US$0.6787), a new closing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par low. *It hit another intra-day low of around C$1.4740 (US$0.6784) in morning\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trade, and the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy CAD at levels of C$1.4730,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.4735 and C$1.4740. *CAD was firmer vs yen as the USD stayed above 146 yen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and 1.81, attracting safe-haven buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 15 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended at a record closing low of C$1.4739 (US$0.6785) on Monday after hitting a new intra-day low of C$1.4740 (US$0.6784) in the morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to weaken after the official closing at 1600 EDT/2000 GMT in Canada, hitting a record low of C$1.4747 (US$0.6781) in late North American spot trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bank of Canada has taken out at C$1.4745 (US$0.6782) again, doing it at every five points, which is the way they always do, generally," one trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In morning trade, there was a wave of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars, every five points from C$1.4730 (US$0.6789) up to C$1.4740 (US$0.6784), which helped slow the Canadian dollar's slide.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One trader estimated the size of each set of purchases by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at US$40-US$50 million, a size that is not unusual for its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite a slight pullback, the overall outlook for the Canadian unit remained bleak as concern over Asia's economic problems continued to spur safe-haven capital flows into U.S. dollar assets. Soft commodities prices also overhung Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Until you start to see it (the U.S. dollar) come off below C$1.4700 (US$0.6803) to figure, I think people with long U.S. dollar (positions) will keep them," another trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The battered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , however, firmed against the Japanese yen in cross trading, rising to 99.10 yen from 98.35 yen late on Friday here. It was the highest level against the yen since early 1993.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen has been hit by growing concern over the stagnant Japanese economy and Asia's financial problems.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the German mark, the Canadian dollar shed earlier gains and was quoted around 1.2275 marks, down from 1.2311 marks in late North American trade on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "There have been some flows coming out of Europe to sell \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     And the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has helped slow down the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar's one-way rise, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "If it weren't there, the dollar/Canada would be a bit higher, probably at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.4760 (US$0.6775) or so," he said, adding that corporate buying of Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par would emerge at that level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Traders and analysts expect the Bank of Canada to hold off from raising its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par key lending rate to boost the local dollar by attracting investors through\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par higher returns on Canadian assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "In respect to other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (than the U.S. dollar), we are performing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par as expected, we're not reaching crisis proportions," said David Ebata, senior\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian market analyst at Technical Data in Boston. "This is an external\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par factor. The Bank of Canada can just sit in the sidelines and watch."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market consensus seems to be that they (the Bank of Canada) won't do\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par anything any time soon," said Reid Farrill, executive director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last raised its bank rate by 50 basis points to 5.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent on January 30 after the Canadian dollar had plunged on speculative\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par selling and spurred monetary conditions that were considered too loose.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By the bank's measure, a weaker Canadian dollar means more stimulus in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par domestic economy as it lowers export prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Canada remained under pressure from soft commodities prices, which have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par also hit the Australian and New Zealand dollars. Canada's Asian exposure is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par more limited than Australia's or New Zealand's, but it is vulnerable to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par volatility in those \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of a perception it shares a similar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dependency on commodities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Australian dollar was quoted around US$0.5792, down from US$0.5885 in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par late Friday trade here, and the New Zealand dollar around US$0.4959, down from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$0.5003.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Key economic indicators for the week:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Tuesday, June 16\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    U.S. May consumer price index (CPI) forecast +0.2 percent after +0.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April, and the core rate (ex-food and energy) +0.2 percent after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par +0.3 percent in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     U.S. May industrial output forecast at +0.4 percent after +0.1 percent in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Canada's April Manufacturing shipments forecast +0.8 percent after +0.9\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Thursday, June 18\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Canada's April merchandise trade surplus forecast C$1.69 billion, up from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.56 billion in March\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Friday, June 19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Canada's May CPI forecast +0.7 percent in all items year on year after +0.8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April, and +1.2 percent in core (ex-food and energy) after +1.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6f02sh1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
