\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends a bit firmer, lacks direction. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4640/1.4710     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4682 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4600 RESISTANCE 1.4750 HIGH 1.4704 LOW 1.4668 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4680 HIGH 1.4700\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4684\par PREV CLOSE  1.4694 LOW  1.4653\tab HIGH 1.4688 LOW 1.4668\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JUNE 12\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended directionless trade a bit firmer, around C$1.4665\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6819). *USD remained firm above 144 yen and 1.80 marks. *Bank of Canada's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary conditions index came in at -5.61 Friday, down from -5.42 a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *U.S. Treasury Secretary Rubin said the yen is weak and the U.S. is concerned.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Japan was confirmed to be in recession, with Q1 GDP down 5.3 pct at an annual\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rate. *U.S. May PPI rose a higher-than-expected 0.2 pct. *Canada's April new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par car sales were up 5.9 pct.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 12 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended nervously firmer at C$1.4665 (US$0.6819) on Friday, licking its wounds after hitting a record low of C$1.4711 (US$0.6798) in active trade on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading lacked direction and was confined in a tight band as players were unsure about how far the Bank of Canada will let the Canadian dollar fall as the U.S. dollar continues to firm against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar, although faced with some profit-taking, stayed afloat, quoted around 144 yen and 1.8050 marks in late North American trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It was a week of safe-haven buying of U.S. dollars and bonds as the global market was increasingly concerned about economic and financial difficulties confronting Asia and Russia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest data confirmed Japan was deep in recession. Its gross domestic product contracted at an annual rate of 5.3 percent in the first quarter after a revised annualized drop of 1.5 percent in the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar has gained sharply also comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Thursday. Rubin urged Japan to deregulate and open markets, which he said is the key to correcting the yen's weakness.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, he said Washington was "very concerned" about the weak yen, but repeated that Tokyo should deal with its financial sector problems.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the U.S. economy appears to be growing steadily without causing serious fears of inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This week's data showed that retail sales rose 0.9 percent in May, stronger than expected, and producer price index came at a higher-than-expected rise of 0.2 percent in May, which was still considered moderate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit edged up to 1.2311 marks from 1.2304 marks at the previous close here, and to 98.35 yen from 98.01 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are getting pessimistic with respect to the commodities prices, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could remain around this level throughout this year, C$1.4600 (US$0.6849) to C$1.4700 (US$0.6803)," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist, macro economics at Bank of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expects commodities prices to stay depressed for now as the gloomy outlook for Japan's economic turnaround will remain a drag on other Asian economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Without sound domestic economic fundamentals - low inflation and government's effort to balance the federal budget this year - the Canadian unit could have fallen more quickly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are positives out there, but in the near term they are just limiting the marked depreciation of the Canadian dollar, Ferley said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy, he said: "The weakness we've seen in the Canadian dollar reflects more of a pessimistic outlook for commodities prices... and they should stay on the sidelines and accept a sort of a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rather than by countering it by raising interest rates."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs Canada, also urged the\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold off on raising rates just to defend the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And he predicts the Canadian dollar should recover to the C$1.4200\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.7042) level in three to six months if commodities prices recover, noting\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that forward contracts for crude oil are pointing to an upward move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was no indication of the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par support the local dollar on Thursday and Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it (the Bank of Canada) might let it (the Canadian dollar) go a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par little bit more," said Harvinder Kalirai, economist at I.D.E.A. in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise its key lending rate if the Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unit weakened to around C$1.4825 (US$0.6745), which would push the monetary\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par conditions index (MCI) down to what is seen as a crucial level of -6.0.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank said the MCI fell to -5.61 on Friday from -5.42 a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials say there's no absolute target range for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the MCI, economists expect the bank to see -6.0 on the MCI's scale, record low\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par levels, as too stimulative for the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kalirai compares the recent decline in the Canadian dollar with the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par situation in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The comment by Bonin was quite interesting that monetary conditions were\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par broadly appropriate. The similarity to January was quite remarkable," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of Canada Senior Deputy Governor Bernard Bonin on Thursday said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's monetary policy should remain on hold for the next five months,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par barring any blows to the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We continue to believe that, between now and the publication of the next\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monetary Policy Report, due in November, the appropriate course would be to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par keep monetary conditions within their recent range, provided there are no new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par shocks," the bank executive said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MCI was at -5.71 on January 21, when Bonin suggested that the bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par might be shifting to less tightening in light of the Asian crisis, while the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par index was at -5.70 on Thursday, when Bonin indicated that there would be no\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par immediate rate increase, Kalirai said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bonin's comments in January sent the Canadian dollar sharply down,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par resulting in a rate hike by the bank on January 30 aimed at staving off a panic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sell-off of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, the Canadian dollar was slowly recovering from the record low of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.4711 (US$0.6798) hit on Thursday, but it still faces downward pressure if\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par commodities prices fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada has come under pressure from soft commodities prices, which have\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par also hit the Australian and New Zealand dollars. Canada's Asian exposure is\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par more limited, but it is vulnerable to volatility in such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a perception it shares a similar dependency on commodities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar is not benefiting much from the recent declining trend\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in its current account deficit. Canada has a better record of trimming the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par negative gap, which shows capital outflows, than the Australian and New Zealand\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Australian dollar was quoted around US$0.5885, and the New Zealand\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar around US$0.5003 in North American trade. Both had fallen to 12-year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lows this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Key economic indicators for next week:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tuesday, June 16\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. May consumer price index (CPI) forecast +0.2 percent after +0.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April, and the core rate (ex-food and energy) +0.2 percent after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par +0.3 percent in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. May industrial output forecast at +0.4 percent after +0.1 percent in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April Manufacturing shipments forecast +0.8 percent after +0.9\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thursday, June 18\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April merchandise trade surplus forecast C$1.69 billion, up from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.56 billion in March\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday, June 19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's May CPI forecast +0.7 percent in all items year on year after +0.8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April, and +1.2 percent in core (ex-food and energy) after +1.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6c037pv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
