\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar hits low,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sits back. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masaru Sato \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1998\par 22:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 11 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar's plunge to a new low against its U.S. counterpart on Thursday is prompting debate on what strategy the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should use to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has customarily tried to defend its dollar by boosting key lending rates, which in theory will attract investors by providing higher returns. \par \par \uc2  But the bank has come under fire since last year for this approach because of fears it may choke off economic growth by driving up borrowing costs for consumers and businesses. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar slipped to a new intra-day low of C$1.4710 (US$0.6798) on Thursday as the U.S. dollar powered ahead against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of safe-haven capital flows from Asia into the United States. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far not moved to raise interest rates to defend the dollar, and did not even appear to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Senior Deputy Governor Bernard Bonin on Thursday said Canada's monetary policy should remain on hold for the next five months, barring any blows to the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We continue to believe that, between now and the publication of the next Monetary Policy Report, due in November, the appropriate course would be to keep monetary conditions within their recent range, provided there are no new shocks," he told financial planners in Quebec City. \par \par \uc2  He said while the most recent figures suggest that Canada's economic outlook is somewhat more favourable than anticipated a couple of months ago, "the fact remains that this outlook carries with it a larger margin of uncertainty than normal." \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's sensible to block every exchange rate move every time," said Andrew Spence, senior economist at Deutsche Bank Securities in Toronto. "By the end of the day, the Bank of Canada is targeting inflation, not the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Canada's inflation rate is below that of the United States, and at the low end of a 1-3 percent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  target band. \par \par \uc2  "If the exchange market remains relatively orderly, they will do nothing," Spence said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely only to "moderate the overshoot" in Canadian dollar selling, instead of blocking speculative selling. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, one of the most powerful figures in the world when it comes to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has said repeatedly that no country can manipulate exchange rates for a sustained period. \par \par \uc2  "Should the bank react to the weakening Canadian dollar? I think the short answer is no," said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs Canada. \par \par \uc2  It would be inconsistent for the bank to do so because it has already said in its May 13 semi-annual policy report that uncertainties from Asia's crisis will prompt wider fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary conditions, he said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, he said, a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts as a shock absorber for natural resources exporters in Canada as cheaper export prices should partly offset softening commodities prices due to slumping Asian demand. \par \par \uc2  Chandler called on the bank to "keep the gunpowder dry." \par \par \uc2  "There are times when you want to raise rates, more so for policy reasons than for short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations," he said. \par \par \uc2  A Canadian rate increase looks unlikely in any case because there is no sign of overheating in the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think the bottom line is that as long as the (U.S.) Fed is on hold, they don't want to go ahead," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  "If the Fed goes (and raises rates), that means that the U.S. economy hasn't slowed down on its own and that should spill over into strong growth in Canada, and that will give the bank reason to tighten," he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said on Wednesday that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs no tightening of monetary policy yet. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has come under pressure from soft commodities prices, which have also hit the Australian and New Zealand dollars. Canada's Asian exposure is more limited, but it is vulnerable to volatility in such \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a perception it shares a similar dependency on commodities. \par \par \uc2  Canada's exports to Asia account for only 2 percent of its gross domestic product, Chandler noted. \par \par \uc2  The Asian exports-to-GDP ratio is 14 percent for New Zealand and 11 percent for Australia. \par \par \uc2  Spence said the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level over the last month or so "is entirely consistent with where relative commodities prices have gone." \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8100, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6b035pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
