\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian markets hit by Asia wes, commodities. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Edmonds \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1998\par 20:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 11 (Reuters) - Canadian equity markets racked up another day of steep losses and the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit record lows on Thursday as the Asian flu and ailing commodity prices gave investors the chills. \par \par \uc2  U.S. equities, hit hard by warnings about corporate profits and fears about the sinking Japanese yen, only worsened Canada's sickly performance. \par \par \uc2  Analysts warned that the bloodletting is unlikely to end soon although most stopped short of predicting a true bear market. \par \par \uc2  "It's not going to stop now. There's still some downside here and it's not going to feel good," said John Kellett, vice-president of Canadian equities at Royal Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not truly bearish insofar as I think the economic fundamentals are reasonably good still." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened to a new all-time intraday low against the U.S. dollar of around C$1.4707 (US$0.6799), traders said. The previous intraday trading low of C$1.4686 (US$0.6809) was set on January 29 this year, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced strong speculative selling. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar is rapidly turning into the Canadian half-dollar," said Fred Ketchen, senior vice-president and director of equity trading at ScotiaMcLeod Inc. \par \par \uc2  The tumble in Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed a surge in the U.S. dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as safe-haven capital flows from Asia into the United States. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin told the U.S. Senate banking committee that while the weak yen is a global concern, the remedy lies in hands of Japanese policymakers. Traders took Rubin's comments as an indication the G7 is taking no joint action to help stem the yen's slide, although Rubin later said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not off the table and U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come at the appropriate time. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Senior Deputy Governor Bernard Bonin said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to be lifting rates to defend the beleaguered Canadian dollar any time soon. \par \par \uc2  "We continue to believe that, between now and the publication of the next Monetary Policy Report, due in November, the appropriate course would be to keep monetary conditions within their recent range, provided there are no new shocks," Bonin told financial planners in Quebec City. \par \par \uc2  The Toronto Stock Exchange, Canada's largest, was under heavy fire from investors as well. At 1340 eastern time, the bellwether TSE 300 Composite Index was down more than 127 points, or 1.72 percent, at 7306.00. Volumes were fairly thin as nervous investors hugged the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  Losses since Monday's close so far total 251 points or roughly 3.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  None of the TSE's 14 sub-sectors was immune to the selling but the gold and silver group was the biggest loser, falling more than five percent by early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  In contrast to the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets, Canadian bonds continued to firm to record highs, profiting along with U.S. treasuries from safe-haven buying. \par \par \uc2  Weak commodity prices have bedevilled the resource-heavy Toronto stock market in recent weeks and analysts said they see little to lift prices on the horizon. \par \par \uc2  In London, gold melted through $290 versus a morning fix of $292.90 an ounce as buyers evaporated from the market. \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's MMS technical analyst Katherine Beatty said she sees little that will immediately reverse Toronto's downward trek. But she still called the fall a correction rather than a true shift of sentiment to bearish from bullish. \par \par \uc2  "A 400-to 500-point correction from 7800 is not a big deal," Beatty said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.47 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6b035gn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
