\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 17:19\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for a second time Wednesday to\par sell U.S. dollars at C$1.4660 in an effort to smooth out\par trading action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The U.S. dollar was at C$1.4660 at 11:14 a.m. EST (1514\par GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after intraday moves in either direction of\par about 70 ticks or more. The U.S. dollar closed at C$1.4586\par Wednesday.\par    The first round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came shortly after 10:00\par a.m. EDT (1400 GMT) as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold U.S. dollars at\par C$1.4655. Traders reported that it subsequently followed its\par usual practice of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at every 5 ticks.\par    The Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been under sustained negative\par pressure in recent weeks from a variety of factors,\par including commodity prices pushed lower by the continued\par economic and financial tumult in Asia and sustained negative\par yield spreads with the U.S.\par    The U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar climbed persistently higher early\par Wednesday morning, approaching its all-time high against the\par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of C$1.4684.\par    Some analysts contend it is essential for the Bank of\par Canada to raise rates to bring an end to the persistent\par weakness in the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  signalled in its semi-annual\par monetary policy report in May that current monetary\par conditions will likely be appropriate for the next six\par months, Governor Gordon Thiessen has also said the bank\par would defend the Canadian dollar in the event of a "crisis\par in confidence" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    The day after the U.S. dollar hit its historic high of\par C$1.4684 on Jan. 29, the Bank of Canada raised its\par trendsetting bank rate 50 basis points to 5.00%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-10-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1119EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du6a01gp8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
