\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends weaker,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,095 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 22:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FORECAST RANGE   1.4600/1.4680     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4625 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4500 RESISTANCE 1.4700 HIGH 1.4633 LOW 1.4579 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4624 HIGH 1.4675\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4626\par PREV CLOSE  1.4588 LOW  1.4620\tab HIGH 1.4633 LOW 1.4583\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WEDNESDAY, JUNE 10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: WEAKER ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended weaker around C$1.4662 (US$0.6820) after Bank of\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slowed U.S. dollar buying. *BOC bought CAD for USD at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par various levels from C$1.4655 (US$0.6824) to C$1.4675 (US$0.6814). *CAD record\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par low vs USD is 1.4686 (US$0.6809) marked on Jan. 29 this year. *U.S. and Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par long bonds surged on Greenspan comments that Fed needs no credit tightening\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par yet. *Market is awaiting any hint of concerted G7 action to stem the yen's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par fall. *USD firm, but slipped from earlier levels vs yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, June 10 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended weaker at C$1.4658 (US$0.6822) after rounds of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pulled it back from a day's low of C$1.4675 (US$0.6814).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in morning trade to buy Canadian dollars at five-point intervals from C$1.4655 (US$0.6824) to C$1.4675 (US$0.6814).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As the U.S. dollar surged against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on safe-haven buying, the Canadian unit continued to slip after opening at C$1.4624 (US$0.6838), down from Tuesday's close at C$1.4588 (US$0.6855).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, however, the Canadian dollar rose to 1.2244 marks from 1.2201 marks at the previous close here, and to 96.46 yen from 96.12 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was marching towards 142 yen and 1.80 marks against the backdrop of safe-haven capital flows into the United States from Asia's troubled economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar hit a record low versus the U.S. dollar at C$1.4686 (US$0.6809) on January 29 this year, when it came under speculative selling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said on Wednesday the Fed has not yet seen a need to tighten its monetary policy, but that it is watching inflation closely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This propelled North American long bond buying but had little impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Greenspan also said the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might need to raise interest rates if U.S. demand does not abate "noticeably."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Fed chief said the U.S. economy is expanding strongly despite a strong drag on trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He was testifying on monetary policy and the outlook for the U.S. economy before a joint economic committee of Congress.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The U.S. dollar should continue to rise to the C$1.4686 (US$0.6809) level,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and hold initially before pulling back to 1.4600/25 (US$0.6849/0.6837), said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Martin Autotte, technical analyst at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "I think there's a fair chance to test C$1.4700 (US$0.6803)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par probably by the end of the week," he said. "As long as we stay above C$1.4600\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from here on, the market is going to stay very strong (for the U.S. dollar)."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The next question is whether, and at what \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par would be forced to raise key interest rates to attract investors to Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets by offering higher yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "I don't think there's a magic threshold. People have said C$1.4600 was a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par magic level, which was pretty speculative," Steven Butler, associate director,\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at Scotia Capital Markets.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have said the bank, before taking monetary policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par action, is watching to see if speculative forces in the market are taking the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down drastically and whether the market is losing confidence\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The officials said the bank would not react to daily fluctuations and they\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par are looking at overall monetary conditions. A weaker Canadian dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par contributes to looser monetary conditions in Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Now that the Australian dollar and the Japanese yen have been sinking to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par new lows, speculator focus might be shifting to the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market has ignored Canada's fairly good economic fundamentals, steady\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par growth with low inflation, and instead followed the flow of buying U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollars\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The strong U.S. jobs data and relatively weak Canadian employment numbers\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par released last Friday triggered the selling of Canadian dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "We are better than Australia, but that's no comfort to Canadians," said a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of Canada source. "I do feel that the (Canadian) dollar should be worth a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par little bit more."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Australian dollar was falling towards US$0.58 to the U.S. dollar, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rebounded to trade around US$0.5882.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Economic fundamentals have a hard time asserting themselves in the face of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par safe-haven capital flows into U.S. dollar-denominated assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Asian sharemarkets tumbled overnight on the growing fear of a falling yen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and its impact on the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market tends to link the image of an Aussie dollar sell-off to Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par selling, although Australian exports are much more exposed to the economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par slump in Asia than Canada's exports to the region. Only eight percent of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's exports go to the Southeast Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market is also looking for any sign of concerted action by the Group of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Seven (G7) nations to stem the yen's fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     G7 deputy ministers are in Paris this week discussing the Russian and Asian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par situations while attending meetings of the Organisation for Economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cooperation and Development.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     A weak yen might be of help for Japan's stagnant economy as it makes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese exports cheaper, but that is a threat for exporters from other Asian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par countries. Meanwhile, the strong U.S. dollar cuts import bills and helps curb\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the strength of the U.S. economy, the market expects slightly stronger\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par retail sales numbers for May on Thursday. Economists on average forecast a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0.7-percent rise in May after a 0.5-percent rise in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the inflation outlook, the U.S. producer price index for May will be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par released on Friday. Economists on average expect both the overall and core PPI\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to rise 0.1 percent after a 0.2-percent rise in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6a03bxu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
