\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds end mixed, long end up on Greenspan. \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 22:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 10 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds ended mixed on Wednesday with the long end of the yield curve sharply firmer on comments by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief that it's not time yet for a U.S. credit tightening. \par \par \uc2  The short end remained weak as the Canadian dollar continued to slide against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy might need to tighten if demand were to continue to exhibit few signs of abating noticeably," U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan told a congressional committee. \par \par \uc2  But he added: "We... have not perceived to date the need to tighten policy in response to strong demand." \par \par \uc2  In early trade, North American long bonds rose on the notion of save-haven capital flows into the United States as Asian share markets tumbled and the Australian dollar was sold overnight on growing concern over the falling Japanese yen and its impact on the region. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen might help Japan's exports, but that is a threat to the exports of other Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  The market is awaiting any sign of concerted action by the Group of Seven (G7) major nations to stem the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  G7 deputy finance ministers are discussing Russia and Asia at a meeting of the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development in Paris this week. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond surged C$0.65 to C$135.44 to yield 5.531 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. 30-year counterpart jumped 41/32 to yield 5.70 percent. The U.S.-Canada spread narrowed to 17 basis points from 23 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  "We have some surprising strength related to the difficulties in Asia last night. The overnight session was quite friendly to North American treasuries, Canada's long end in particular," said Jeoff Hall, managing Canadian market analyst at Technical Data in Boston. \par \par \uc2  This prompted the Ontario provincial government to reopen sales of its 6.5 percent C$500 million long bond, maturing March 8, 2029, which was well received, he said. \par \par \uc2  Dominion Bond Rating Service said it is assigning a rating of A (high) to this bond issue. \par \par \uc2  "So we have a good market tone going to important data later in the week," Hall said. \par \par \uc2  On the strength of the U.S. economy, the market expects slightly stronger retail sales numbers for May on Thursday. Economists on average forecast a 0.7-percent rise in May after a 0.5-percent rise in April. \par \par \uc2  On the inflation outlook, the U.S. producer price index for May will be released on Friday. Economists on average expect both the overall and core PPI to rise 0.1 percent after a 0.2-percent rise in April. \par \par \uc2  The short end of the Canadian yield curve remained under pressure as the Canadian dollar was weakening. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar ended weaker at C$1.4658 (US$0.6822) on Wednesday after rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  dredged it back from a day's low of C$1.4675 (US$0.6814). \par \par \uc2  "The curve continues to flatten as investors bail out of 2-to 3-year bonds into the long end," Hall said. \par \par \uc2  The Government of Canada said its C$3.5 billion auction of 5.0 percent bonds due December 1, 2000 produced the average yield of 5.229, priced at 99.480. \par \par \uc2  The high/low gap was 14 basis points and the tail (high vs average) was 9 points. \par \par \uc2  The money market was weaker as the Canadian dollar continued to fall against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The T-bill yield has been relatively low recently and given the weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the current level of MCI, the market is perhaps looking for a Bank of Canada rate hike," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would adjust money in circulation by raising interest rates if its monetary conditions index (MCI) swings widely to show excessively loose conditions. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 4.83 percent after 4.73 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6a03bw3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
