\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar fall spurs  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 23:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 10 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar continued to slide on Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market tested the resolve of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By the end of the day the Canadian dollar was still slouching towards its record low of C$1.4686 to the U.S. dollar (US$0.6809), marked on January 29 this year. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day it came under speculative selling by U.S. dollar bulls, who braved a wave of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This helped stabilise the Canadian unit, which closed at C$1.4658 to the U.S. dollar (US$0.6822) on Wednesday. It opened at C$1.4624 (US$0.6838), down from Tuesday's close at C$1.4588 (US$0.6855). \par \par \uc2  The next question is whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to raise interest rates to make the return on Canadian assets attractive enough to foreign investors. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said that before raising rates they want to see if speculative forces in the market are spurring a loss of confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They have said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not react to daily fluctuations and is looking at overall monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  Canada is not the only loser in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market these days. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar has been hitting 12-year lows recently, while the Japanese yen fell to 141.67 yen to the U.S. dollar and the German mark slipped to 1.7990 marks on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has been surging against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  because worries over economic problems in Asia and Russia have prompted renewed safe-haven buying of U.S. assets. These assets are considered safe due to the economic and military might of the United States. \par \par \uc2  Flight-to-quality capital flows to the United States have spilled over into Canada's long-term bonds, but have not helped the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  With the Australian dollar and the Japanese yen at low levels, speculators have appeared to be looking for a new victim. In doing so, they have ignored Canada's fairly good economic fundamentals, steady economic growth and low inflation. \par \par \uc2  Also, strong U.S. job growth and relatively weak Canadian employment numbers released last Friday triggered selling of the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We are better than Australia, but that's no comfort to Canadians," said a Bank of Canada source. "I do feel that the (Canadian) dollar should be worth a little bit more." \par \par \uc2  Australian exports are much more exposed to the economic slump in Asia than are Canada's. Less than 10 percent of Canada's exports go to Southeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8100, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6a03bts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
