\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends weaker, lacks direction. \b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 1998\par 22:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4510/1.4580\tab    ASIAN CLOSE   1.4544\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RESISTANCE   1.4450 SUPPORT 1.4600     HI 1.4558  LO 1.4525\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4533   HI 1.4575\tab   EUROPE OPEN   1.4528\par PREV CLOSE   1.4539   LO 1.4525\tab   HI 1.4549  LO 1.4525\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, MAY 29\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL. ACTIVITY: MODERATE *Canada dollar ended weaker around C$1.4566 ($0.6865) after dealers tested USD's topside up to C$1.4575 (US$0.6861). *No clear direction for CAD, which opened a bit firmer, gaining on crosses overnight. *USD pared gains vs mark as worries over Russia's financial system and Germany's exposure to it receded.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, May 29 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended weaker around C$1.4566 (US$0.6865) on Friday after dealers tested the topside of the U.S. dollar in early trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was left in lackluster trading while the world focused on safe-haven flows into the United States in the face of the financial turmoil in Russia, uncertainties in Asia, heightened tension between India and Pakistan, and U.S.-Japan tolerance of a weakening yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shed most of its earlier gains in cross trading, particularly against the mark, as worries over Russia's financial stability and Germany's exposure to it receded later.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early trade, some players took U.S. dollar short positions against the Canadian unit. Canada's gain was limited to C$1.4525 (US$0.6885).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Nobody really was seen buying U.S. dollars, so made into Aussie/Canada buying..., but basically there were stops at C$1.4550/55 (US$0.6873/70) and just got cleaned up.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar's advance was also capped at C$1.4575 (US$0.6861).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the crosses, the Canadian dollar pared earlier gains. It was quoted at 1.2255 marks, up a bit from 1.2234 marks at the previous close here, and at 95.22 yen, down from 95.38 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think the market sentiment is that they expect Canada to weaken further," the dealer said. "The market is getting more fun selling Canada than buying it, testing the (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central) bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resolves to see if they are really going to do anything."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to trade in the range of C$1.4450-1.4650 (US$0.6920-0.6823) for the next month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar lacked clear direction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the last day and a half, the slide (in the Canadian dollar) has abated, but we're basically sitting here around the C$1.4540 (US$0.6878) area, waiting to see what's next," said Reid Farrill, executive director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's hard to say what's really going to be the next factor to move the market."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has told the market that there is no absolute level for the monetary conditions index (MCI), which the market was watching for signs of bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or tightening aimed at defending its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Gordon Thiessen has repeatedly said there is no target zone for the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar has not recovered from selling that was sparked by the market perception that the the bank would leave its accommodative monetary policy unchanged for the next six months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers were disappointed by the May 13 release of the bank's semi-annual policy report, which did not contain a much-sought-after bias towards credit tightening.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar slipped after touching 139 yen overnight as caution over a Bank of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased fears Japan's deepening economic problems. Japan's jobless rate hit a record high of 4.1 percent in April, creeping up to the unemployment rate in the United States, which has fallen to 4.3 percent in the latest data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The average forecast for Canada's key economic indicators for next week:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Q1 current account deficit seen falling by C$5.51 billion after a decline of C$6.24 billion in the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Q1 GDP seen rising 2.3 percent after a 0.7-percent rise in the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par May jobless rate seen at 8.4 percent, unchanged from April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Employment growth seen at 14,300 after 71,700 in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5t04083\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
