\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends firmer, but still weak. \b0\par\par\uc2 956 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1998\par 22:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4450/1.4550\tab    ASIAN CLOSE   1.4480\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RESISTANCE   1.4400 SUPPORT 1.4590     HI 1.4512  LO 1.4447\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4502   HI 1.4516\tab   EUROPE OPEN   1.4500\par PREV CLOSE   1.4445   LO 1.4463\tab   HI 1.4498  LO 1.4450\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, MAY 14\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH. ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *The Canadian dollar ended a bit firmer than its opening level,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par quoted at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.4470 (US$0.6911), but still weaker than its previous closing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par here. *Trading\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par was cautious ahead of the May 19 FOMC. *Selling started\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday when the BOC\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expressed a neutral monetary policy in semi-annual report. *U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April CPI came\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in higher than expected: up 0.2 pct overall, up 0.3 pct for core\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rate. *U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stats official says it's too early to say inflation bottomed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par out, except in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par some areas. *Canada April CPI rate of increase was lower than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expected: up 0.8\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par pct y/y after 0.9-pct rise in March, and down 0.1 pct m/m after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a 0.1-pct rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Core CPI up 1.2 pct y/y after 1.3-pct rise in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, May 14 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar shed some overnight losses to end at C$1.4470 (US$0.6911) on Thursday, but was still weaker than yesterday's closing level here.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading is cautious ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy-setting meeting next Tuesday and a long weekend in Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Selling of the Canadian, which broke out on Wednesday over Canada's neutral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, appeared to have lost steam.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par No sign of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was seen today as some players tested the U.S. dollar topside.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think they (the bank) disappointed a few people. The market was looking to try take longer at C$1.4520, but that failed to materialize," said one dealer. "People wanted to test their resolve."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Basically corporate Canada was met by sell-Canada throughout the day," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was stop-loss U.S. dollar selling interest at C$1.4525-1.4555 (US$0.6885-0.6870), he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar rose versus the mark, while easing against the yen on fears of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A bigger-than-expected rise in the key measure of U.S. inflation hurt the U.S. dollar slightly in morning trade. The U.S consumer price index (CPI) rose 0.2 percent overall and was up 0.3 percent the core rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the mild inflationary uplift signaled by these numbers and the U.S. producer price index Wednesday, economists see the U.S. Federal Reserve as keeping its monetary policy on hold for the time being.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An statistics official at the U.S. Department of Labor said it was too early to say whether prices had bottomed out.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's key inflation rate has stayed at the low end of the bank's 1-3 percent target band, and is unlikely to move substantially higher in the coming months, economists said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Inflation remains very low in Canada...It certainly isn't going to help the Canadian dollar. It basically implies that the Bank of Canada has little reason to consider an increase in rates over the near term," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at MMS International.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "So given that, and that conditions remain unsettled in Asia, and that the market believes in that environment, the Canadian dollar is going to underperform."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unrest in Indonesia's capital of Jakarta continued, and India's nuclear tests are heightening tension with its neighbors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The numbers are coming in a little bit weaker than expected, so suggesting no need for policy to address an inflation problem," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist, macro economics at Bank of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The negative tone in the Canadian dollar emerged yesterday and continued overnight. That's the risk. This could be another factor to continue to weigh on the Canadian dollar," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's April CPI rate of increase was lower than expected: up 0.8 percent year on year after a 0.9-percent rise in March, and down 0.1 percent month on month unadjusted for seasonal factors after a 0.1-percent rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The core CPI was up 1.2 percent year on year after 1.3-percent rise in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada's neutral money policy, unveiled in its semi-annual monetary policy report on Wednesday, triggered a sell-off on Wednesday. The bank sought to stem the fall by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday, but no sign of action was seen this morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Looking ahead, Canada's March merchandise trade data due\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par out next Wednesday are expected to post an improvement,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par possibly leading to a narrower deficit of C$5 billion in the Q1\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par current account balance, Palombi said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "So that would take us back from the three percent of GDP\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in current account deficit to roughly to 2.5 percent. That\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par could be good news for the Canadian dollar," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "Overnight we got hit because the U.S. dollar lost ground\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a result of Asia. But safe-haven\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par flows could easily go back into the U.S. dollar, provided the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trade data next week is good," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     On the crosses, the Canadian dollar recovered from this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par morning's levels, but was still quoted lower at 1.2287 marks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par after 1.2308 marks at the previous close here. Canada also cut\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par some losses against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but was still weaker\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par at 92.37 yen after 92.80 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5e058da\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
