\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 16:51\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again Wednesday morning, selling\par U.S. dollars at C$1.4445 shortly around 10:30 a.m. EDT (1400\par GMT) as part of a continued effort to smooth out trading\par action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.4410 after the\par U.S. dollar surged immediately after the release of the Bank\par of Canada's semi-annual monetary policy report at 9:30 a.m.\par EDT (1330 GMT). The U.S. dollar jumped from around C$1.4350\par before the release to the C$1.4400 range shortly before\par 10:00 a.m. EDT.\par    Traders said the bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly after the\par Canadian dollar started plunging, with the most recent round\par coming at C$1.4445. The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4425\par at 10:40 a.m. EDT (1440 GMT).\par    The crucial element in the report was a statement that\par the current level of monetary conditions will be appropriate\par for the next six months, given the prospect of slowing\par economic growth, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    That statement was interpreted as suggesting that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not be increasing interest rates soon.\par Some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market players had been speculating that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise interest rates in defense of the\par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which weakened again in April.\par    Many analysts believe the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will stay\par weak as long as Canadian interest rates remain lower than\par those in the U.S.\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after intraday moves in either direction of\par 70 ticks or more in order to contain volatility. The U.S.\par dollar closed at C$1.4340 Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  05-13-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1051EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du5d01908\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
