\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar takes dive on neutral bank policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masaru Sato \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 23:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, May 13 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar tumbled on Wednesday after Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  telegraphed in its semi-annual monetary report that it was not looking to cool economic growth with higher interest rates this year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost over one Canadian cent to the U.S. dollar before settling around C$1.4445 (US$0.6923). \par \par \uc2  It took a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying millions of Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, before the selling subsided. \par \par \uc2  In its semi-annual monetary policy report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said recent monetary conditions in Canada would be "broadly appropriate" for the next six months. \par \par \uc2  This caught many traders off guard as they had expected the bank to indicate a need to tighten the money in circulation probably by the end of the year, a move usually taken to ensure a sustainable pace of growth. \par \par \uc2  "The short end of the (bond) market is in a little bit of trouble because people are worried now that the Bank of Canada maybe will let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  go a little bit weaker," said Jim Webber, director of fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. "It doesn't mean that they won't ever be raising rates, but it's difficult to figure out when." \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. dollar is receiving underlying support from a widely expected shift to a tightening bias by the Federal Reserve, the Canadian dollar is finding no such comfort north of the border. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of higher interest rates usually props the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as capital tends to move to high-yielding assets. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen at a news conference refused to discuss whether the Canadian dollar was undervalued against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  But he said underlying economic fundamentals are strong for the Canadian dollar as the inflation rate is lower than in the United States and commodities prices are expected to pick up. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  predicted the economy would grow at 3.5 percent in 1998, which is still one of the best performances among major industrial nations, but slower than 4.0 percent, forecast by the bank six months ago. \par \par \uc2  Canada's inflation should remain in the lower half of the target range of 1-3 percent for the next six months, the bank said. The main risks for the Canadian economy are the impact from Asia's financial crisis, particularly on the western part of the nation, and soft commodities prices. \par \par \uc2  Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at MMS International, warned that the Bank of Canada's neutral position does not necessarily mean it will not act at all before the next report is published in November. \par \par \uc2  "If Asia doesn't weigh down the economy and the U.S. economy doesn't slow down, then there will be a reason for the bank to raise before the next monetary policy report," he said. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen's comment that "monetary conditions may continue to fluctuate over a relatively wide range" drew some attention in the market. \par \par \uc2  "This suggests that a weakening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not necessarily prompt an offsetting hike in interest rates if the weakness was thought to be transitory," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist, macro economics at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  After this morning's sell-off, some see support for the battered Canadian unit. \par \par \uc2  Susan Stearns, director, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Montreal in New York said she would go for long positions for the Canada dollar at C$1.4465 (US$0.6913). \par \par \uc2  Randall Powley, senior economist at Scotia Capital Markets, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should rebound and move in a relatively narrow range of C$1.4200-1.4400 (US$0.7042-0.6944) for the next two months until strong buying incentive emerges. \par \par \uc2  But in the very near-term, the Canadian dollar still has a tainted image. \par \par \uc2  "You don't want to be long Canada," said a senior dealer at a Canadian bank. "The market's still angry about the report." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5d05e4q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
