\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends sharply down on BOC report. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4330/1.4475\tab    ASIAN CLOSE   1.4349\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RESISTANCE   1.4250 SUPPORT 1.4490     HI 1.4361  LO 1.4346\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4348   HI 1.4455\tab   EUROPE OPEN   1.4344\par PREV CLOSE   1.4342   LO 1.4343\tab   HI 1.4355  LO 1.4343\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WEDNESDAY, MAY 13\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH. ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada dollar ended sharply lower around C$1.4450 (US$0.6920),\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par losing over one\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian cent to USD. *The Bank of Canada's neutral monetary\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par policy stance,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expressed in its semi-annual report, triggered a sell-off in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par morning. *BOC\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stepped in and first bought CAD for USD at C$1.4410 (US$0.6940)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and continued\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at various levels up to possibly C$1.4450\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6920). Next data\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to watch: Canada's April CPI (+1.0 pct y/y vs 0.9 pct in March)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par due out at 0700\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EDT/1100 GMT and U.S. CPI (+0.2 pct vs flat) at 0830 EDT/1230\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *BOC gov Thiessen told briefing Canada's fundamentals are strong\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for CAD, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par refused to say whether it's undervalued vs USD. *BOC said recent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par conditions would be "broadly appropriate" for next six months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *U.S. retail\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sales rose slightly higher than expected, core PPI within\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expectations. *U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April retail sales +0.5 pct (fcast +0.3 pct), ex-autos +0.5 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (+0.5 pct),\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par April PPI +0.2 pct (+0.1 pct), core rate +0.2 pct (+0.2 pct).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, May 13 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended sharply lower around C$1.4450 (US$0.6920) on Wednesday after the Bank of Canada's indication of a neutral monetary policy stance triggered a sell-off in the morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A wave of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to slow the pace of selling, but the near-term outlook is still uncertain.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lost over one Canadian cent to the U.S. dollar before settling around the closing level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You don't want to be long Canada," said a senior dealer at a Canadian bank. "The market's still angry about the report."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He sees the dollar moving in a range of C$1.4435-1.4475 (US$0.6928-0.6908) overnight.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to have stepped in at various levels at C$1.4410 (US$0.6940), C$1.4415 (US$0.6937), C$1.4425 (US$0.6932), C$1.4430 (US$0.6930), C$14440 (US$0.6925), and possibly C$1.4450 (US$0.6920.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If we go above C$1.4455 (US$0.6918), I think they (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) will come in around C$1.4460/65 (US$0.6916/13) again," another dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In its semi-annual monetary policy report, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said recent monetary conditions in Canada would be "broadly appropriate" for the next six months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This caught many traders off guard as they had expected the bank to indicate a need to tighten the money in circulation to guide the economy into a sustainable pace of growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank now predicts the Canadian economic growth will be at 3.5 percent in 1998, still good but lower than its previous forecast of 4.0 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's inflation should remain in the lower half of the target range of 1-3 percent for the next six months, it said. The main downside risks for Canada are Asia's financial crisis and soft commodities prices, it added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the U.S. dollar is receiving underlying support from expectations of a change in Federal Reserve bias toward credit tightening, the Canadian dollar lacks such support at the moment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Expectations of higher interest rates usually support a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as capital tends to move to high-yielding assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opened at C$1.4348 (US$0.6970) today, little changed from Tuesday's North American closing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After this morning's sell-off, some see support for the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par battered Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Susan Stearns, director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Bank of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Montreal in New York,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said she would go Canada long at C$1.4465 (US$0.6913) today.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Randall Powley, senior economist at Scotia Capitals Markets,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said the\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should rebound and move in a range for the next two\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par months until a strong buying incentive emerges.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On how to read the report, Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par analyst at MMS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par International, warned that the Bank of Canada's neutral position\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par does not\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par necessarily mean it will not act on interest rates before the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par next report is published in November.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If Asia doesn't weigh down the economy and the U.S. economy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par doesn't slow\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par down, then there will be a reason for the bank to raise (rates)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par before the next monetary policy report," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to come under pressure in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par near term, and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market should seek further direction from the spread between\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian and U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par yields, said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The key phrase in the conclusion calls for neutral monetary\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par conditions for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the next six months," he said. "Something certainly changed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par their view of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par world, maybe productivity increases."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Earlier in the day, key U.S. inflation data showed slightly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par higher-than-expected rises, but U.S. inflation is still\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par considered benign.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. April retail sales rose 0.5 percent (consensus forecast\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par +0.3 percent),\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ex-autos up 0.5 percent (+0.5 percent). April producer price\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par index (PPI) was up\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0.2 percent (+0.1 percent), the core rate up 0.2 percent (+0.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists told Reuters Television that it was hard to say\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par whether U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation was on an upward trend just by looking at these\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par numbers, and thus the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was unlikely to raise rates at its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par policy-setting committee meeting next Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's March new motor vehicle sales rose 5.0 percent,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par compared with a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consensus forecast of an increase of 6.5 percent. It was the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par second straight\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par gains after a decline in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, Canada's April consumer price index (+1.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent y/y vs 0.9\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in March) will be released at 0700 EDT/1100 GMT and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. CPI (+0.2 percent vs flat) at 0830 EDT/1230 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the crosses, the Canadian dollar shed earlier gains and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par fell to 1.2308\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par marks from 1.2382 marks at the previous close here, and slipped\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to 92.80 yen from 93.31 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar, as a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was buoyed by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par jitters over Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par India's nuclear tests provoked more outrage around the world,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and the tension\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with neighboring Pakistan heightened. A clash between Indonesian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par security\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par forces and demonstrators resulted in the deaths of six students\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in Jakarta on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5d05dyw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
