\par\par \b \uc2  Canada's Chretien says wants rates to stay low. \b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 13 (Reuters) - Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien said on Wednesday that his government would like Canadian interest rates to stay low. \par \par \uc2  Chretien told reporters at a ceremony to open a newly refurbished Candian embassy building in London that he believed the country's economic fundamentals were strong and that interest rates should remain low. \par \par \uc2  "We would like to maintain low interest rates...because we have low inflation," Chretien told reporters in London just after Bank of Canada governor Gordon Thiessen released the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's semi-annual policy report. \par \par \uc2  Chretien gave Thiessen credit for Canada's high growth, low inflation economy. \par \par \uc2  "(It was) because the governor of the Bank (of Canada) said there will be 3.25 percent growth in Canada that we had close to four percent growth last year and unemployment is going down," Chretien said inside the newly refurbished Canada House. "So for me I think the Canadian story is pretty good." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bought Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars around C$1.4410 (US$0.6940) on Wednesday as the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened sharply in the wake of the Bank of Canada's report on Canadian economic growth and inflation. \par \par \uc2  In its semi-annual monetary policy report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said recent monetary conditions in Canada would be "broadly appropriate" for the next six months. \par \par \uc2  The Bank also said Canadian economic growth in 1998 would not be as vigorous as thought before, Canada's inflation should remain in the lower half of the target range of 1-3 percent for the next six months, and that the main downside risks for Canada are Asia's financial crisis and soft commodities prices. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada's key rate was last raised 50 basis points to five percent on January 30. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a target range for overnight lending of 4.5-5.0 percent. ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5d05d4t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
