\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds, bills open mixed, curve flattens. \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, April 14 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds and T-bills opened mixed on Tuesday, with the yield curve flattening slightly, as recent weakness in the Canadian dollar weighed on the short end of the curve. \par \par \uc2  "The curve is definitely flattening. I think people are making a bit more of a fuss over yesterday's dollar weakness than they should given the light flows," said Hank Cunningham, director of fixed income with First Marathon Securities Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "Curve-flattening is the major theme here in Canada," he said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year bond fell C$0.06 to C$134.03 to yield 5.613 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. counterpart rose 6/32 to yield 5.92 percent. The negative spread between the two narrowed to 31 basis points from a record 32 basis points at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened sharply to $1.4350 (US$0.6968) in unusually thin trading on Monday, forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar opened flat at $1.4346 (US$0.6970) in light trading on Tuesday as markets continued to speculate that the Bank of Canada will soon raise interest rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  That speculation especially affected the Canadian money market. Canada's three-month when-issued T-bill softening to yield 4.58 percent from 4.57 percent at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness was a catalyst to moving rates a little bit higher," said Walter Posiewko, a money market trader with Royal Bank Investment Management Inc. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be buying all the way through the piece into the weakness, and there doesn't seem to be any panic forming in this market. Apparantly there is cash in the market to be employed," he said. \par \par \uc2  In new issue news, the Bank of Canada plans to auction C$3.0 billion of 21-day treasury bills on April 15, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The bills will be delivered on April 16 and will mature on May 7, the Bank of Canada said. \par \par \uc2  The tender is subject to standard auction terms. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8100, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4e07g43\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
