\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds close weaker, outperform U.S. issues. \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, April 13 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds closed weaker in quiet trading on Monday, pulled lower by softer U.S. treasuries. But they still outperformed their U.S. counterparts despite a slide in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's sort of a weak day in terms of liquidity. The most significant aspect was that the Canadian long bonds actually held in fairly well," said Sheldon Dong, manager of fixed-income research with Midland Walwyn Capital Inc. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year bond fell C$0.19 to C$134.09 to yield 5.610 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. counterpart fell 20/32 to yield 5.93 percent. The negative spread between the two widened to a record 32 basis points from 29 points at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  U.S. treasuries slid much lower amid talk the Bank of Japan was selling bills to raise cash. But Canadian bonds did not match that decline even though Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply. \par \par \uc2  A tight supply of bonds is one of the factors that has been cited for the impressive performance of Canada against the U.S. market. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened sharply to $1.4350 (US$0.6968) in unusually thin trading on Monday, forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Canadian dollars at the C$1.4330 (US$0.6968) level after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swooned on the back of broad-based selling. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's weakness weighed on the short end of the Canadian yield curve especially, with the three-month when-issued T-bill softening to yield 4.57 percent from 4.54 percent at the close of trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8100, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4d07mib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
