\par\par \b \uc2  Canada  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 /Policy -2: May Want Tighter Money \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 20:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and slow down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise this month sent the reverse signal, Goldman's Kasumovich said. \par \par \uc2  The bank's inaction suggests it still wants to see monetary conditions tightened - its stated policy stance since last May - and it would prefer that tightening to come through appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than higher interest rates, Kasumovich said. \par \par \uc2  'I think it's consistent with the view that over a longer time horizon they would like to see tighter conditions, and they would perhaps like to see it through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' he said. 'At the same time, they have brought forward so much of the tightening they had planned to do though '98, even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to stall, we probably wouldn't see a rate hike until the latter part of the year.' \par \par \uc2  Kasumovich and others point to something else that might be keeping the bank out of action when its normal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  point comes up - an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the bank on Jan. 9 that choked off an incipient rally sparked by strong employment numbers. \par \par \uc2  That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew criticism from some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players, who couldn't understand why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would block an upswing when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness had clearly been a concern to bank officials. \par \par \uc2  Andrew Pyle, chief strategist with ABN AMRO Hoare Govett, said the Jan. 9 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  implied the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a base level for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'The market . . . took that as a signal and started to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively,' Pyle said. The Bank of Canada wants to avoid a repeat of that scenario, and that's one reason bank officials are not responding to recent gains by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the usual fashion. \par \par \uc2  'And I think that's quite natural - and I think it's probably very appropriate policy too.' \par \par \uc2  Another factor dissuading the bank from sticking too strictly to its 'normal' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a reduced need to build \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Pyle suggested. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS  03-30-98 \par \par \uc2  01:48 PM \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010916du3u02su3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
