\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Alters  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Policy: Analysts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Don Curren \par \uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be trying to send a signal to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by abandoning its normal policy for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks, some observers say. \par \par \uc2  But exactly what that signal is and how it should be interpreted is something those observers don't agree about. \par \par \uc2  Three times in March, the Bank has abstained from its normal, unofficial policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by buying or selling U.S. dollars against the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the U.S. dollar moves more than 70 ticks in either direction against the Canadian dollar from the previous day's close. \par \par \uc2  The most recent instance occurred on Wednesday, March 25, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged from Tuesday's close of C$1.4164 to C$1.4085. The other two occurred on March 9, which witnessed a jump from C$1.4193 to C$1.4094. and March 2, where it went from C41.4239 to C$1.4160. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials do not comment publicly on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Marcel Kasumovich, an economist with Goldman Sachs, believes that by deviating from its unofficial policy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to send a signal to the market. \par \par \uc2  'In the past, the bank used deviations from its standard, daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rule as a signal of how they feel about the mix of monetary conditions,' Kasumovich said. \par \par \uc2  In December and January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under sustained negative pressure that drove it to the lowest level in its 140-year history. In those months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more frequently than its policy would dictate, selling U.S. dollars in an effort to contain the downward movement. \par \par \uc2  The bank was clearly signaling its disapproval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, according to Kasumovich and other observers. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS  03-30-98 \par \par \uc2  01:47 PM \par \par \uc2  4214 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010916du3u02su1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
