\par\par \b \uc2  = \highlight22\uc2 Canada Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Alters  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Policy: Analysts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Don Curren \par \uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 20:37\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be trying\par to send a signal to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by abandoning its\par normal policy for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in\par recent weeks, some observers say.\par    But exactly what that signal is and how it should be\par interpreted is something those observers don't agree\par about.\par    Three times in March, the Bank has abstained from its\par normal, unofficial policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets by buying or selling U.S. dollars against the\par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the U.S. dollar moves more than 70\par ticks in either direction against the Canadian dollar from\par the previous day's close.\par    The most recent instance occurred on Wednesday, March\par 25, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surged from Tuesday's close of\par C$1.4164 to C$1.4085. The other two occurred on March 9,\par which witnessed a jump from C$1.4193 to C$1.4094. and\par March 2, where it went from C41.4239 to C$1.4160.\par    Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials do not comment publicly on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    But Marcel Kasumovich, an economist with Goldman\par Sachs, believes that by deviating from its unofficial\par policy, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is trying to send a signal to the\par market.\par    "In the past, the bank used deviations from its\par standard, daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rule as a\par signal of how they feel about the mix of monetary\par conditions," Kasumovich said.\par    In December and January, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under\par sustained negative pressure that drove it to the lowest\par level in its 140-year history. In those months, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more frequently than its\par policy would dictate, selling U.S. dollars in an effort to\par contain the downward movement.\par    The bank was clearly signaling its disapproval of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decline, according to Kasumovich and other\par observers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  03-30-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1337EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4214 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du3u00ubf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
