\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Continues  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1998\par 18:41\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday, selling\par U.S. dollars every five ticks after its intial action at\par C$1.4630, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The Canadian dollar continued to sag despite the first\par round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shortly after 11:00 a.m. EST (1600\par GMT), which signalled a new historic low for the Canadian\par dollar. It continues to plunge through new lows despite the\par bank action, and was at C$1.4655 at 12:28 a.m. EST (1728\par GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves 70 ticks or more\par in either direction from the previous day's close. While the\par U.S. dollar closed at C$1.4583 Wednesday, the bank has been\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more frequently and at smaller swings in the\par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value than usual in recent weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Canadian dollar has been under severe downward\par pressure from a variety of factors, including a\par current-account deficit that surged to C$25.5 billion in the\par third quarter of 1997 and sustained negative yield spreads\par with the U.S.\par    The pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intensified last week when\par speeches by Bank of Canada officials were interpreted as\par suggesting the bank would not be raising interest rates soon\par because of uncertainty about the health of the Canadian\par economy. Recent soft economic has reinforced that view.\par    While some analysts contend that it is essential for the\par bank to raise rates to bring confidence and stability back\par to Canadian financial markets, others say it would imperil\par the current economic expansion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  01-29-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1241EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du1t007r7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
