\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Action Comes At All-Time Low \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1998\par 17:39\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday to sell U.S. dollars at C$1.4630 at around 11 a.m. EST (1600 GMT) in an effort to smooth out trading action, said a trader in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level signalled a new historic low for the Canadian dollar. The U.S. dollar levelled off after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was at C$1.4615 at 11:14 a.m. EST (1614 GMT). It subsequently edged back up to C$1.4623 by 11:25 a.m. EST (1625 GMT). \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unofficial policy is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after intraday moves in either direction of 70 ticks or more. While the U.S. dollar closed at C$1.4583 Wednesday, the bank has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more frequently than usual and at smaller swings in the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has been under severe downward pressure in recent weeks from a variety of factors, including a current-account deficit that surged to C$25.5 billion in the third quarter of 1997 and sustained negative yield spreads with the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  intensified last week when speeches by Bank of Canada officials were interpreted as suggesting that the bank would not be raising interest rates soon because of uncertainty about the health of the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  On Jan. 20, Bank of Governor Gordon Thiessen said it is impossible to accurately assess the robustness of the economy because of uncertainty about the impact of the Asian financial crisis and the devastating ice storm that swept through Eastern Canada earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  While some analysts contend that it is essential for the bank to raise rates to bring confidence and stability back to Canadian financial markets, others say it would imperill the current economic expansion. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  01-29-98 \par \par \uc2  1139EST \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du1t007l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
