\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1998\par 21:49\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again\par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Tuesday, selling U.S. dollars\par at C$1.4560 at around 3:00 p.m. EST (2000 GMT) in an effort\par to smooth out trading action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The trader said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par repeatedly in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, beginning with the sale of\par U.S. dollars at C$1.4550 just before 12:00 p.m. EST (1700\par GMT), and then again at C$1.4555 and most recently at\par C$1.4560.\par    The U.S. dollar was at C$1.4555 at 3:26 p.m. (2026 GMT)\par shortly after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Unofficially, the bank tends to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against\par intraday moves in excess of 70 ticks, which with the Monday\par close at C$1.4503, would have put the point around C$1.4570.\par But the bank has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more frequently than usual\par in the last few weeks as the U.S. dollar flirted with\par its previous all-time of C$1.4465 - set on Feb. 4, 1986 - and\par then finally broke through that level last week.\par    The U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar's current all-time high of C$1.4577 was\par recorded early in North American trading last Friday.\par    The U.S. dollar actually dropped sharply against the\par Canadian in the early session, and traded just over C$1.4460\par at around 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT). But a higher-than-expected U.S.\par employment cost increase of 1.0% in the fourth quarter of\par 1997 slammed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back down, prompting the multiple\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    The Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been afflicted by a variety of\par negative pressures in recent weeks, including a worsening\par current account deficit - it reached an annualized rate of\par C$25.5 billion in third quarter - and negative interest rate\par differentials against the U.S.\par    The downward pressure intensified last week when remarks\par by Bank of Canada officials were interpreted as suggesting\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was backing off its stance of tightening\par monetary conditions, and would not be raising interest rates\par soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  01-27-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1549EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du1r0083j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
