\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar sinks to new record lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4505/1.4560\tab     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4463\par RESISTANCE 1.4386   SUPPORT 1.4540\tab HI 1.4475   LO 1.4450\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4469/74    HI 1.4534     EUROPE OPEN    1.4454\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  PREV CLOSE   1.4456/61    LO 1.4450     HI 1.4474   LO 1.4454 \par \par \uc2  ----------------------MARKET COMMENT------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * Canada's dollar weakened to an all-time closing low of C$1.4528 (US$0.6883) in sporadic but heavy trading on Thursday as jittery investors sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on speculation that the Bank of Canada would not raise short-term interest rates, dealers said. * The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in money markets to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar weakened to a record closing low of C$1.4528 (US$0.6883) in sporadic but heavy trading on Thursday on speculation the Bank of Canada would not raise short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "It's ugly out there. Nobody can come up with a reason to buy the Canadian dollar," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice to buy Canadian dollars in money markets, but failed to change the extremely bearish sentiment in the market. \par \par \uc2  Economists said markets were concerned that an apparent policy shift by the Bank of Canada portended a continued gradual erosion against the U.S dollar. \par \par \uc2  The policy change came to light on Wednesday, when Bank of Canada Deputy Governor Bernard Bonin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not uncomfortable with either the level of the dollar or current monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  Bonin's comments triggered a wave of selling in overnight markets that carried over into the North American session. \par \par \uc2  "All that has happened is the rate of change has slowed down, but the direction of change has not," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the release of tame Canadian inflation data today also encouraged markets to keep selling Canada dollars. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada reported that December's unadjusted consumer price index fell 0.1 percent on the month in line with analysts' expectations. \par \par \uc2  "It's not going to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said David Ebata, senior market analyst with Technical Data in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain under pressure. I think the Bank of Canada, quite frankly, realized that this could very well be the case on the inflation side," Ebata said. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Canadian dollar dipped to 1.2407 marks from Wednesday's 1.2574 marks and dipped to 87.63 yen from 87.82 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1m02k48\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
