\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: Cdn Dlr Slips Closer To All-Time Low \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1998\par 22:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar slid perilously close to its all-time low Wednesday, forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to contain its decline. \par \par \uc2  The drop came despite news that Canada's merchandise trade surplus jumped to C$1.03 billion in November from C$432 million in October. Many market watchers attributed it to the lingering influence of a Tuesday speech by Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen, which was interpreted as suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be raising interest rates soon, and a speech on Wednesday from another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official which seemed to reinforce Thiessen's remarks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4449 at 4:34 p.m. EST (1334 GMT), up from C$1.4408 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT), and from C$1.4415 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (While the Bank of Canada officially puts the all-time hgh reached by the U.S. dollar on Feb. 4, 1986, at C$1.4465, some market veterans claim it reached C$1.4480 that day.) \par \par \uc2  'I suspect Thiessen's speech of yesterday had something to do with it, and I guess the other shoe dropped today when (Senior Deputy Governor Bernard Bonin) sort of elaborated on those comments,' said Arve Bendiskrud, fixed-income analyst with T.D. Securities Inc. \par \par \uc2  Bonin, who spoke to financial analysts in Montreal, said negative economic factors such as the financial crisis in Asia and the recent ice storm in Eastern Canada would likely dampen economic growth in 1998. The new economic situation means that a new approach to monetary conditions appears to be called for, he said. \par \par \uc2  He also mentioned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 'not uncomfortable' with current monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  While the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied briefly after the trade report came out at 8:30 a.m. EST (1330 GMT), it began to lose ground again shortly afterwards. The Bank of Canada sold U.S. dollars at around C$1.4450 just after 2:40 p.m. (1940 GMT), which slowed down the downward slide. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 21-01-98 \par \par \uc2  2141GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1l00lpu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
