\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar threatens to touch record low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1998\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 21 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar lumbered toward a record low on Wednesday with no relief in sight as the financial markets gave up hope that the Bank of Canada would raise short-term interest rates anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to 69.19 U.S. cents after opening the North American trading session at 69.47 U.S. cents, a hair's breadth away from the record low of 69.13 U.S. cents established in February, 1986. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has now shed five percent of its value in the past three months. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest slide was triggered by senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials who appeared to pour cold water on market speculation of an imminent interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Deputy Bank of Canada Governor Bernard Bonin said on Wednesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not uncomfortable with the current level of monetary conditions and hinted at a possible change in the bank's "less accommodative" monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "It could be less tightening or it could be more easing," Bonin told reporters after a luncheon speech to Montreal financial analysts. \par \par \uc2  His comments triggered a rapid sell-off of the Canadian dollar, prompting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the 69.20 U.S. cent level. \par \par \uc2  Yesterday, Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen said Asia's economic crisis would likely reduce the bank's growth projections in for the Canadian economy in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "There is no doubt that the events in Asia will dampen our earlier projections of output growth for this year," Thiessen said in a speech to the Ivey School of Business in Kitchener, Ontario. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen later added that Asia's crisis meant that Canada's output gap would not shrink as quickly as expected, putting off prospects of higher inflation in the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think what they're trying to do is send out the signal that they are going to be on the sidelines for a while yet," said Rick Egelton, deputy chief economist with Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "Right now in Canada we have 0.9 percent inflation, below the lower end of the Bank of Canada's one- to three-percent target range, and unemployment is still relatively high, so I think the Bank of Canada has a fair bit of scope before it has to raise interest rates," Egelton said. \par \par \uc2  Attempts by both Theissen and Bonin to downplay the severity of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against the U.S. dollar -- Thiessen underlined the Canadian dollar's relative strength against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , failed to calm jittery markets. \par \par \uc2  "That's like saying my fingers and toes feel fine, but it's the gunshot in my chest that's bothering me," said Jeoff Hall, economist with Technical Data in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Hall said the Canadian dollar could weaken to 68.02 U.S. cents during the next two months as the size of Canada's ballooning current account deficit became more apparent. \par \par \uc2  Along with the worldwide decline in commodity prices, the current account, which is a measure of a country's indebtedness, has been a major factor in the Canadian dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  Canada's third-quarter current account deficit swelled to C$25.5 billion (US$17.6 billion) on an annualised basis. \par \par \uc2  Economists said trade data released on Wednesday, showing continued softness in Canada's merchandise trade surplus, could pave the way for a C$30-billion (US$20.7 billion) current account deficit in the fourth quarter and more trouble for the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1l03xel\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
