\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Bonds End Lower On Weaker U.S. Mkt, Struggling C$ \b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 1998\par 23:08\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds followed the U.S.\par market lower Tuesday, with the short end of the curve\par receiving an extra negative kick from the plummeting\par Canadian dollar.\par    "It's a pretty quiet day," said one trader with a major\par investment firm. "We kind of reacted a little bit to the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first thing in the morning. (Since) then it's been\par the U.S. drifting off through the day that's been more the\par action."\par    4.75s 1999  99.69  dn 0.11  4.94% vs 4.87%\par    5.50s 2002 101.28  dn 0.26  5.18% vs 5.12%\par    7.25s 2007 113.35  dn 0.35  5.41% vs 5.37%\par    8s    2027 132.60  dn 0.25  5.76% vs 5.74%\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-yr: -0.16 vs -0.16\par    CGB MAR 97 125.98  up 0.22 volume 2,488\par    "Lethargy" in the U.S bond market is driving the decline\par in prices there, the trader said. "I think it's just been\par too much sitting around, too many people not playing," he\par said.\par    "We seem to have to a large extent just tracked the\par U.S.," agreed Arve Bendiskrud, fixed-income analyst with\par T.D.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Securities Inc.\par    Bendiskrud also cited concern about the languishing\par Canadian dollar as a force behind the weakening of the short\par end of the bond market. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plunged further into\par territory not seen since early 1986 on Tuesday, reaching\par C$4.4420 in late trading and prompting two rounds of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an\par effort to reduce trading volatility.\par    A lunch-time speech by Bank of Canada Governor Gordon\par Thiessen was another market focus on Tuesday, Bendiskrud\par said.\par    By suggesting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was backing off its\par initial optimistic projections for growth in the Canadian\par economy in 1998, the speech may have created the impression\par the bank would be less prone to raise interest rates, he\par said. "That would tend to give a pretty negative tone to the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  01-20-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1708EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1182 4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020317du1k00abz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
