\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar closes weaker after Thiessen talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4380/1.4435\tab     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4384\par RESISTANCE 1.4310   SUPPORT 1.4415\tab HI 1.4411   LO 1.4381\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4397/02    HI 1.4419     EUROPE OPEN    1.4380\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  PREV CLOSE   1.4385/90    LO 1.4365     HI 1.4406   LO 1.4380 \par \par \uc2  ----------------------MARKET COMMENT------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * Canada's dollar closed weaker at C$1.4415 (US$0.6937) in steady trading on Tuesday after Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen said that Asia's financial crisis would have a detrimental impact on the Canadian economy, traders said. \par \par \uc2  * The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the C$1.4415 (US$0.6937) and C$1.4420 (US$0.6934) levels. \par \par \uc2  Those remarks touched off market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was downplaying the possibility of an imminent interest rate hike, triggering a selloff of the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It shook people up a little bit because they're looking at that and saying, 'Okay, if he's saying that, then that means he's not going to raise rates more,'" said Andrew Pyle, economist with ABN-AMRO Hoare Govett. \par \par \uc2  "That is why the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains weak," Pyle said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice in money markets to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the Canadian dollar at the C$1.4415 (US$0.6937) and C$1.4420 (US$0.6934) levels, but failed to stave off the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to a weaker range. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Canada unit was now positioned to make a run at its 12-year low of C$1.4465 (US$0.6913). \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't look good," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  "I can't imagine anybody being long Canada," the trader added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move would depend on key Canadian trade date due to be released on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey of economists predicted that Canada's merchandise trade balance would be C$700 million in November, up slightly from C$600 million in October. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Canadian dollar dipped to 1.2746 marks from Monday's 1.2761 marks and slipped to 88.99 yen from 89.59 yen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1k03vg9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
