\par\par \b \uc2  Weaker Canadian dollar pulls bonds down by close. \b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 20 (Reuters) - A weaker Canadian dollar dragged bonds down to close lower on Tuesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  play again," said Larry Berman, technical analyst at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities Inc. The unit weakened to finish at a low of C$1.4415 (US$0.6937) after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gordon Thiessen said Asia's financial crisis would have a detrimental effect on the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year benchmark bond fell C$0.35 to C$131.57 to yield 5.758 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its U.S. counterpart fell 16/32 to yield 5.84 percent and the negative spread between the two widened to eight basis points from seven basis points at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Short-term treasuries weakened sharply in response to the dollar's drop. Canada's three-month when-issued T-bill gave up ground to yield 4.37 percent from Monday's 4.22 percent. "There is no doubt that events in Asia will dampen our earlier projections of output growth for this year," Theissen told a Kitchener, Ontario audience. \par \par \uc2  His words sparked debate on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would opt for an interest rate hike to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  "Our short-end guys are looking for the bank to move fairly soon," Berman said. \par \par \uc2  Canadian fixed-income instruments also fell in concert with the U.S. market, which was hurt by a softer U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Hank Cunningham, fixed-income director at First Marathon Securities. \par \par \uc2  Market movers will be watching Statistics Canada trade figures for November due to be released on Wednesday. A Reuters survey of economists forecast the merchandise trade surplus would rise to be C$700 million from C$600 million in October. \par \par \uc2  In other prices, the Canadian 10-year bond futures contract trading on the Montreal Exchange fell 0.22 to 125.98 with 2,488 contracts changing hands. A total of 616 contracts changed hands on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Lydia Zajc, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8109)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1k03vg2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
