\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Down In Thin Trade; Some See Rate Hike \b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 1998\par 22:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar was weaker late Monday as domestic players took advantage of thin conditions caused by a U.S. holiday to have a run at key support levels. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4376 at 4:26 p.m. EST (2126 GMT), up from C$1.4363 at 7:00 a.m. EST (1200 GMT), and from C$1.4366 late Friday. It peaked at C$1.4388 at 11:15 GMT, putting it dangerously close the 12-year high at C$1.4415 hit in December. A break of that level leaves room for a test of C$1.4465, the all-time high hit Feb. 4, 1986. \par \par \uc2  'I'm surprised the market took advantage of the U.S. holiday to run this thing up,' said Andrew Pyle, chief strategist with ABN Amro Hoare Govett. An easing of tensions in Asia and a series of strong economic data here ought to have boosted the Canadian dollar by now. \par \par \uc2  But 'maybe it needs a rate hike to rally,' Pyle said, suggesting that is just what may occur, and soon. With a close above C$1.4375, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  point based on its unofficial rule of standing against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves of 70 ticks would stand at C$1.4445. But the bank may just raise rates instead. 'I can't see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  letting the (U.S. dollar) through C$1.4415,' said Pyle. \par \par \uc2  Barring a rate hike in the normal operating time around 9:00 a.m. EST on Tuesday, the market will turn its attention to a luncheon speech in Kitchener, Ontario by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gordon Thiessen. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Canadian dollar faces a key test in the November trade figures. There's concern exports will continue to lag imports, narrowing the trade surplus or even sending into a deficit for the first time since 1976. That would bode poorly for the current-account deficit, which already widened dramatically in the third quarter, and the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:26 p.m. EST (2126 GMT), 7:00 a.m.\par EST (1200 GMT) and late Friday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.4376     1.4363     1.4366\par    CDN/DEM     1.2767     1.2824     1.2759\par    CDN/YEN     89.80      89.87      89.93\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-01-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2130GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1j00il8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
