\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Continues At C$1.4215 \b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 15:47\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Tuesday to buy U.S. dollars at\par C$1.4225 at around 9:30 a.m. EST (1430 GMT) in an effort to\par smooth out trading action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    Reports are that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par as the U.S. dollar got down to C$1.4220 and then C$1.4215.\par    The Canadian dollar began rallying earlier Friday on\par news the domestic economy churned out 61,700 in December,\par well above economists forecasts. The unemployment rate fell\par to 8.6%, putting it below 9% for the first time since\par September 1990.\par    The U.S. dollar closed Thursday at C$1.4312, and reached\par a low at C$1.4212. As an unofficial policy, the Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market whenever the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves\par 70 ticks or more in either direction, largely just to\par discourage excessive volatility.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  It is not expected to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily, however, for fear standing in the way of\par the Canadian dollar's recovery from recent heavy losses, say\par traders.\par    Since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.S. dollar traded at\par C$1.4216 at 9:43 a.m. EST.\par \f28 \par \par 