\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Rally Fizzles Out, Despite Strong Jobs \b0\par\par\uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 22:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--A show of strength in Canada's December labor market put some muscle on the Canadian dollar earlier Friday, but it couldn't sustain it in the face of an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weak stocks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4300 at 4:00 p.m. EST (2100 GMT), up from C$1.4253 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT) and from C$1.4312 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  'I'm really surprised its back here after this morning's rally,' said a dealer with one of the major Canadian banks. 'Everyone got all bulled up when the U.S. dollar fell through C$1.4285 and then reached C$1.4215,' he said. But there the Bank of Canada stepped in to buy U.S. dollars, and 'I don't think anyone knows why,' he added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to stand against intraday moves in excess of 70 ticks, but often doesn't. This time it chose to enter against the Canadian dollar at C$1.4225. \par \par \uc2  After a brief attempt to resume its rally, the Canadian dollar succumbed to profit taking, and eventually the move downward triggered a series of stop-loss orders to buy the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada set the agenda early with news of a huge 61,700 new jobs in December, far exceeding even the most optimistic forecasts. Excluding part-time jobs, which fell 49,100, full-time employment was up a stunning 110,800. Combined with a drop in the number of active job seekers, that knocked the unemployment rate down to 8.6%, from 9.0%, a level not seen since September of 1990. \par \par \uc2  Those numbers suggest the economy has entered a phase of 'sustained upswing based on income growth,' said Andrew Spence, senior economist with Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. Just judging by robust car sales in December, it seems consumers are in a mood to spend that won't likely change should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nudge interest rates still higher in the period ahead, he said. That ought to prove supportive of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 09-01-98 \par \par \uc2  2104GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du19008wv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
