\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar closes firmer in erratic trading. \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4275/1.4335\tab     ASIAN CLOSE    1.4353\par RESISTANCE 1.4240   SUPPORT 1.4390\tab HI 1.4355   LO 1.4318\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4313/18    HI 1.4351     EUROPE OPEN    1.4340\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  PREV CLOSE   1.4315/20    LO 1.4280     HI 1.4355   LO 1.4318 \par \par \uc2  ----------------------MARKET COMMENT------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * The Canadian dollar closed stronger at C$1.4290 (US$0.6997) in erratic afternoon trading on Wednesday after investors showed interest in buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  * Trading activity is expected to remain quiet and relatively rangebound heading into the key U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due to be released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar closed stronger at C$1.4290 (US$0.6997) in erratic afternoon trading on Wednesday after several large funds showed interest in buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Somebody came in and bought their whack of Canada and now they're going to ride it," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  The surge of buying helped to offset some of the losses experienced by the Canadian dollar on Tuesday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swooned to under C$1.4325 (US$0.6980) forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it by buying Canadian dollars. \par \par \uc2  Yesterday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came on the heels of a three-day rally which saw the Canadian dollar strengthen to C$1.4236 (US$0.7024) early this week. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada is trying to dampen the speculation that has fueled Canada dollar trading, said David Ebata, Canadian analyst with Tech Data. \par \par \uc2  "It is working to some extent," Ebata said. \par \par \uc2  But continued weakness in Asian markets and a deep slide in commodity prices will make it difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the Canadian dollar into a stronger range, Ebata said. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.2765 marks from Tuesday's close of 1.2808 marks and dropped to 92.42 yen from 93.19 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du170183n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
