\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar closes weaker in light trading. \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4375/1.4405\tab     ASIAN CLOSE     1.4362\par RESISTANCE 1.4278   SUPPORT 1.4428\tab HI 1.4369   LO  1.4357\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN  1.4371/76    HI 1.4398     EUROPE OPEN     1.4356\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  PREV CLOSE   1.4353/58    LO 1.4357     HI 1.4356   LO  1.4370 \par \par \uc2  ----------------------MARKET COMMENT------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * The Canadian dollar continued its downward trend on Monday closing at C$1.4393 (US$0.6947) in light trading after opening the session at C$1.4371 (US$0.6958), traders said. * They said corporate selling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted repeated Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the C$1.4395 (US$0.6946) level. \par \par \uc2  "The markets are not very kind to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Obviously, there are not a lot of players out there. Most of the interest is coming from interbank and corporate interest to buy (U.S.) dollars," said Stephen Wateridge, managing director of corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with CIBC Wood Gundy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that markets would continue to test the Canadian dollar this week and place the Bank of Canada in the difficult position of having to consider further interest rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its key bank rate by 75 basis points since late November in an effort to defend the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "This is a continuation of very negative sentiment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a loss of confidence and the almost consensus view that unless interest rates are raised or the Bank of Canada shows a much more aggressive stance to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the fall will continue," said Andrew Pyle, economist with ABN-AMRO Hoare Govett. \par \par \uc2  Even news that U.S. investment firms were willing to help back the bailout of the South Korean economy failed to put a positive spin on the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We saw bullish news in Asia. One would have presumed that would give the Canadian dollar some support," Pyle said. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, as much as people like to go around saying that all of this is because of Asia, today was evidence that this is not the correct assessment," Pyle said. \par \par \uc2  Pyle did not rule out a test of the Canadian dollar's 11-year low of C$1.4464 (US$0.6913) during the next few weeks. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Canadian dollar rose to 1.2429 marks from 1.2357 marks at Friday's close and slipped to 89.89 yen from 90.87 yen on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: paul.simao@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtct00khz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
