\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar seen sinking near record low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lydia Zajc \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1997\par 23:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The ailing Canadian dollar, under siege by speculators, is expected to decline toward its all-time low in coming weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts predicted on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  With Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently loath to raise interest rates for fear of aborting an economic recovery, the Canadian dollar is seen slipping as low as 69.13 U.S. cents (C$1.4465). \par \par \uc2  At that level, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value would match the lowest level ever reached against the U.S. dollar -- a nadir last seen on Feb. 4, 1986. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed on Tuesday at 69.54 U.S. cents (C$1.4380) in North American trading. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets late in the session to bolster the Canadian dollar, buying it for U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  This year the Canadian dollar, known locally as "the loonie" after the picture of a wild water fowl stamped on the flip side of the coin, dropped more than 5 U.S. cents from a high of 74.85 U.S. cents (C$1.3366) in late January. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a barometer of Canada's export and commodity-driven economy, has been buffeted for weeks by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators and further undermined by a fall in gold bullion and base metal prices to their lowest levels in years. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the Asian economic flu could spread to Canada have further pressured the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada recently has tried in vain to shore up the dollar by twice raising short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The last time it boosted the key bank rate -- similar to the U.S. Federal funds rate -- was on Dec. 12, raising it 50 basis points to 4.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since held off from more rate hikes, leading some market watchers to speculate that it was prepared to let the unit weather the storm until most market players returned from the Christmas and New Year holidays. \par \par \uc2  Others have argued that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling U.S. dollars to buy Canadian dollars, and let the unit drift lower to find a natural level supported by Canada's strengthening economy and improving fiscal picture. \par \par \uc2  "The speculators are testing the Bank of Canada's resolve," said Harv Kalirai, I.D.E.A. analyst in New York. "If you do get (to) C$1.44 (69.44 U.S. cents)) I think another rate hike will be sooner rather than later." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcn00nhi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
